   110	  treatment.
   111	
   112	**What fails in the product.** Lindel&ouml;fness of $\mathbb{R}_\ell$ rests on the rationals being dense and at most countable, so that a cover can be thinned to countably many rational-endpoint intervals plus countably many exceptional points. In the square the antidiagonal is a set on which every basic box is a single point, and there are uncountably many of those points; no countability of the rationals helps, because the boxes never contain two of them at once.
   113	
   114	**Neither compactness nor Lindel&ouml;fness is what separates the topologies.** $\mathbb{R}_\ell$ is finer than the usual topology of $\mathbb{R}$, since every $(a,b)$ is a union of half-open intervals, and both spaces are Lindel&ouml;f and not compact; the difference shows up only in the square.
