FILE items/def-cantor-set.md
28: ## Definition
29: 
30: For $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ write
31: 
32: $$\tfrac{1}{3} S \;:=\; \{\, x \cdot 3^{-1} : x \in S \,\}, \qquad \tfrac{2}{3} + \tfrac{1}{3} S \;:=\; \{\, 2 \cdot 3^{-1} + x \cdot 3^{-1} : x \in S \,\},$$
33: 
34: and let $F : \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}) \to \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R})$ be
35: 
36: $$F(S) \;:=\; \tfrac{1}{3} S \ \cup \ \big(\tfrac{2}{3} + \tfrac{1}{3} S\big).$$
37: 
38: By the recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]), applied to the set
39: $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R})$, the starting element $[0,1]$ ([[def-interval]]) and
40: the function $F$, there is a unique family $(C_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of subsets
41: of $\mathbb{R}$ with
42: 
43: $$C_0 = [0,1], \qquad C_{n+1} = F(C_n) = \tfrac{1}{3}C_n \cup \big(\tfrac{2}{3} + \tfrac{1}{3}C_n\big) \quad (n \in \mathbb{N}).$$
44: 
45: The **Cantor middle-thirds set** is
46: 
47: $$C \;:=\; \bigcap_{n \in \mathbb{N}} C_n .$$
48: 
49: **The first step really is the removal of the open middle third.** Directly from
50: the clauses,
51: 
52: $$C_1 \;=\; \tfrac{1}{3}[0,1] \cup \big(\tfrac{2}{3} + \tfrac{1}{3}[0,1]\big) \;=\; [0, \tfrac13] \cup [\tfrac23, 1] \;=\; [0,1] \setminus (\tfrac13, \tfrac23),$$
53: 
54: the middle equality because $x \mapsto x \cdot 3^{-1}$ is an order isomorphism of
55: $\mathbb{R}$ onto itself with inverse $x \mapsto 3x$ ([[def-ordered-field]],
56: [[lem-of-sign-rules]]), and the last because $0 \le x \le 1$ splits, by
57: totality of the order, into $x \le \tfrac13$, $\tfrac13 < x < \tfrac23$ and
58: $x \ge \tfrac23$. The recursion then performs the same operation inside each of
59: the two scaled copies, which is what "removing the open middle thirds" names.
60: 
61: **Every $C_n$ lies in $[0,1]$**, by induction on $n$ ([[thm-induction-principle]]):
62: $C_0 = [0,1]$; and if $C_n \subseteq [0,1]$ then $\tfrac13 C_n \subseteq [0,\tfrac13]$
63: and $\tfrac23 + \tfrac13 C_n \subseteq [\tfrac23, 1]$, so
64: $C_{n+1} \subseteq [0,1]$ ([[lem-of-add-order]], [[lem-of-sign-rules]]). The same
65: computation shows that **the two halves of $C_{n+1}$ are disjoint**, the first
66: lying in $[0,\tfrac13]$ and the second in $[\tfrac23,1]$, and $\tfrac13 < \tfrac23$
67: ([[cor-of-one-positive]]).
68: 
69: **The family is nested**, $C_{n+1} \subseteq C_n$ for every $n$, again by
70: induction. For $n = 0$ this is $C_1 = [0,\tfrac13] \cup [\tfrac23,1] \subseteq
71: [0,1]$. And $F$ is monotone, in the sense that $S \subseteq T$ implies
72: $F(S) \subseteq F(T)$, directly from the displayed description of $F$; so
73: $C_{n+1} \subseteq C_n$ gives $C_{n+2} = F(C_{n+1}) \subseteq F(C_n) = C_{n+1}$.
74: Consequently $C = \bigcap_n C_n \subseteq C_m$ for every $m$, and
75: $\bigcap_n C_{n+1} = \bigcap_n C_n = C$.
76: 
77: **Powers.** Here $3^{-n}$ means $(3^{-1})^n$, the integer power of
78: [[def-integer-power]], so that $3^{0} = 1$, $3^{-(n+1)} \cdot 3 = 3^{-n}$ and
79: $3^{-n} > 0$ for every $n$ ([[lem-power-laws]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
80: 
FILE items/def-compact-space.md
30: ## Definition
31: 
32: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]).
33: 
34: - An **open cover** of $(X,\mathcal{T})$ is a family
35:   $\mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ of open sets with $X = \bigcup \mathcal{U}$,
36:   where $\bigcup \mathcal{U} = \{\, x \in X : x \in U \text{ for some } U \in \mathcal{U} \,\}$.
37: - A **subcover** of $\mathcal{U}$ is a subfamily $\mathcal{V} \subseteq \mathcal{U}$
38:   that is itself an open cover.
39: - A family $\mathcal{V}$ of sets is **finite** when $\mathcal{V} = \varnothing$ or
40:   there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and sets $V_0, \dots, V_n$ with
41:   $\mathcal{V} = \{V_0, \dots, V_n\}$; repetitions in the list are allowed and
42:   harmless.
43: - $(X,\mathcal{T})$ is **compact** when every open cover of it has a finite
44:   subcover: for every open cover $\mathcal{U}$, either $X = \varnothing$ and the
45:   empty subfamily covers it, or there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
46:   $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with
47:   $$X = U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n .$$
48: - A subset $A \subseteq X$ is a **compact subset** of $X$ when the subspace
49:   $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is a compact topological space, $\mathcal{T}_A$ being the
50:   subspace topology ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
51: 
52: **Compactness of a subset is defined intrinsically, and only intrinsically.**
53: The last clause speaks about the subspace $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ and its own open
54: sets, not about families of open subsets of the ambient $X$. The two readings do
55: agree, but that is a theorem and not a convention: it is
56: [[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]], and no item of this library may use
57: the ambient reading without citing it. Taking the intrinsic reading makes
58: compactness a property of the topological space $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ alone.
59: Hence it is preserved when $A$ is embedded homeomorphically as a subspace, or
60: when another ambient space induces the same topology on $A$; it need not be
61: preserved if the induced topology changes. This is exactly the convention already fixed for
62: metric spaces by [[def-metric-compactness]], and the agreement of that
63: definition with this one is [[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]].
64: 
65: **The empty space is compact**, since the empty subfamily of any family covers
66: it; this is the reason the clause above is written with the two cases. Every
67: space listed as $\{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$ is compact too: given a cover, each $x_i$
68: lies in some member, and finitely many members named in this way already cover.
69: So every finite space is compact, whatever its topology, and in particular the
70: discrete topology on a finite set is compact while the discrete topology on an
71: infinite set is not ([[def-standard-topologies]]).
72: 
73: **The finiteness convention.** "Finite" above is the listing form. It agrees with
74: the definition of finiteness by equinumerosity with a natural number
75: ([[def-countable]]), in both directions, and the agreement is the one already
76: discharged in [[def-metric-compactness]]: a nonempty set equinumerous with
77: $m \ge 1$ is listable, and a set listed as $\{a_0, \dots, a_n\}$ injects into
78: $\sigma(n)$ by sending $x$ to the least $i \le n$ with $a_i = x$. Neither
79: direction uses a choice principle; the second selects nothing, taking a least
80: index instead.
81: 
82: **Quasicompact is not used here.** Some authors, following Bourbaki, reserve
83: *compact* for a space that is both compact in the above sense and Hausdorff, and
84: call the open-cover condition alone *quasicompact*. This library follows the more
85: widely adopted convention: **compact** means the open-cover condition and nothing
86: more, and a Hausdorff hypothesis is always written out. The fork is recorded in
87: [[rem-compactness-conventions-and-choice-ledger]].
88: 
FILE items/def-compactness-variants.md
41: ## Definition
42: 
43: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), with
44: open covers, subcovers, finiteness and compactness as in [[def-compact-space]],
45: and *finite*, *at most countable* and *uncountable* as in [[def-countable]].
46: 
47: - $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **countably compact** when every open cover of $X$ that
48:   is at most countable has a finite subcover.
49: - $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **Lindel&ouml;f** when every open cover of $X$ has an at
50:   most countable subcover.
51: - $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **sequentially compact** when every sequence $(x_k)$ in
52:   $X$, that is every function $x : \mathbb{N} \to X$
53:   ([[def-sequence-convergence-top]]), has a subsequence $(x_{n_j})$ converging to
54:   a point of $X$, the index map $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ being strictly
55:   increasing ([[def-sequence]], [[lem-index-map-grows]]).
56: - $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **limit point compact** when every infinite subset
57:   $A \subseteq X$ has a limit point in $X$, that is a point $p \in X$ every
58:   neighbourhood $N$ of which satisfies $N \cap (A \setminus \{p\}) \ne \varnothing$
59:   ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]). Here *infinite* means not finite in
60:   the sense of [[def-countable]].
61: - $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **$\sigma$-compact** when there is an at most countable
62:   family $\mathcal{K}$ of compact subsets of $X$ with $X = \bigcup \mathcal{K}$.
63: - A subset $A \subseteq X$ is **relatively compact in $X$** when its closure
64:   $\overline{A}$ ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]) is a compact subset of
65:   $X$.
66: 
67: A subset $A \subseteq X$ is called countably compact, Lindel&ouml;f,
68: sequentially compact, limit point compact or $\sigma$-compact when the subspace
69: $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]), exactly as for
70: compactness. **Relative compactness is the exception and is deliberately not of
71: that form**: it is a statement about $A$ *inside* $X$, since $\overline{A}$ is
72: computed in $X$, and a set may be relatively compact in one space and not in
73: another that contains it. Every other notion on this list is intrinsic to the
74: subspace.
75: 
76: **The countable covers may be listed.** A nonempty at most countable family
77: $\mathcal{U}$ admits a surjection $\mathbb{N} \to \mathcal{U}$
78: ([[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]]), so countable compactness says: for
79: every sequence $(U_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of open sets with
80: $X = \bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} U_n$ there are finitely many indices whose sets
81: already cover $X$. That surjection is produced from the countability assumption
82: alone and no choice principle is involved; the empty family covers only the empty
83: space, which is compact anyway.
84: 
85: **Indexing starts at $0$.** A sequence here is a function on $\mathbb{N}$ and
86: $\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$ ([[def-sequence]]), so a subsequence is
87: $(x_{n_j})_{j \in \mathbb{N}}$ with $n_0 < n_1 < \cdots$ and $n_j \ge j$
88: ([[lem-index-map-grows]]). An index range taken from a text that starts at $1$
89: must be shifted before it is used here.
90: 
91: **Agreement with the metric definitions.** Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space carrying
92: its metric topology $\mathcal{T}_d$ ([[def-metric-topology]],
93: [[def-metrizable-space]]). Then the three notions that
94: [[def-metric-compactness-variants]] defines metrically are the three defined
95: above, read in $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$:
96: 
97: - *Countably compact.* The open sets used there are the members of
98:   $\mathcal{T}_d$, so the at most countable open covers are the same families and
99:   the condition is the same condition, exactly as for compactness itself
100:   ([[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]]).
101: - *Sequentially compact.* Convergence of a sequence in the metric sense and in
102:   the sense of [[def-sequence-convergence-top]] agree on a metric topology,
103:   because the balls around a point are a neighbourhood base at it
104:   ([[def-metrizable-space]]); the subsequences quantified over are the same.
105: - *Limit point compact.* A point $p$ is a limit point of $A$ in the metric sense
106:   when every ball around $p$ meets $A \setminus \{p\}$, and in the sense above
107:   when every neighbourhood does; the same neighbourhood base makes the two
108:   conditions one ([[def-metrizable-space]],
109:   [[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]).
110: 
111: So no statement below about a metrizable space introduces a second notion, and
112: every theorem of the metric development about these three properties may be
113: quoted here once a metric inducing the topology is named. Lindel&ouml;fness,
114: $\sigma$-compactness and relative compactness have no metric counterpart in this
115: library and are defined here for the first time.
116: 
FILE items/def-complete-ordered-field.md
29: ## Definition
30: 
31: Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \subseteq F$.
32: 
33: - $u \in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \le u$ for all $s \in S$; $S$ is
34:   **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.
35: - $u \in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$
36:   is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.
37: 
38: $F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the
39: **least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty
40: $S \subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.
41: 
FILE items/def-continuous-map-top.md
32: ## Definition
33: 
34: Let $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ and $(Y, \mathcal{T}_Y)$ be topological spaces
35: ([[def-topological-space]]), let $f : X \to Y$ be a function and let $x \in X$.
36: Neighbourhoods are as in [[def-neighbourhood-top]].
37: 
38: $f$ is **continuous at $x$** if for every neighbourhood $V$ of $f(x)$ in $Y$ the
39: preimage $f^{-1}[V]$ is a neighbourhood of $x$ in $X$.
40: 
41: $f$ is **continuous** if it is continuous at every point of $X$.
42: 
43: **The same condition with open sets only.** $f$ is continuous at $x$ if and only
44: if for every open $V \subseteq Y$ with $f(x) \in V$ there is an open
45: $U \subseteq X$ with $x \in U$ and $f[U] \subseteq V$. Indeed, if $f$ is
46: continuous at $x$ and $V$ is such an open set, then $V$ is a neighbourhood of
47: $f(x)$, so $f^{-1}[V]$ is a neighbourhood of $x$ and contains an open $U \ni x$,
48: which satisfies $f[U] \subseteq V$. Conversely, given the displayed condition and
49: a neighbourhood $V$ of $f(x)$, fix open $V_0$ with $f(x) \in V_0 \subseteq V$ and
50: then open $U \ni x$ with $f[U] \subseteq V_0$; then $x \in U \subseteq f^{-1}[V_0] \subseteq f^{-1}[V]$,
51: so $f^{-1}[V]$ is a neighbourhood of $x$. Both forms are used below and are the
52: same statement written twice.
53: 
54: **Preimage, not image.** $f^{-1}[V] = \{\, x \in X : f(x) \in V \,\}$
55: is the preimage in the sense of [[def-injection-surjection-bijection]] and is
56: defined for every function, invertible or not; no inverse function is being
57: asserted to exist. Continuity is a condition on preimages throughout, and the
58: corresponding conditions on images define the *open* and *closed* maps of a later
59: item, which are different notions.
60: 
FILE items/def-countable.md
43: ## Definition
44: 
45: Recall that a natural number is a von Neumann natural
46: ([[def-natural-numbers]]): $0 = \varnothing$ and $\sigma(n) = n \cup \{n\}$, so
47: that
48: 
49: $$n = \{\, m \in \mathbb{N} : m < n \,\} = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$$
50: 
51: is itself the set of its predecessors. Here $<$ is the order of
52: [[def-nat-order]], which is defined additively, so the displayed identity is a
53: theorem and not a convention: it is [[lem-nat-order-is-membership]], proved
54: immediately above. Let $A$ be a set, and let $\approx$ be equinumerosity
55: ([[def-equinumerous]]).
56: 
57: - $A$ is **finite** if $A \approx n$ for some $n \in \mathbb{N}$.
58: - $A$ is **countably infinite** if $A \approx \mathbb{N}$.
59: - $A$ is **at most countable** if it is finite or countably infinite.
60: - $A$ is **uncountable** if it is not at most countable.
61: 
FILE items/def-countable-choice.md
42: ## Definition
43: 
44: The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, is the following
45: statement.
46: 
47: > For every family $(X_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by
48: > $\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\mathbb{N}$ such that
49: > $f(n) \in X_n$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$.
50: 
51: Equivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most
52: countable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.
53: 
FILE items/def-hausdorff-space.md
37: ## Definition
38: 
39: A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$ ([[def-topological-space]]) is
40: **Hausdorff** when any two distinct points are separated by disjoint open sets:
41: for all $x, y \in X$ with $x \ne y$ there are $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$ with
42: 
43: $$x \in U, \qquad y \in V, \qquad U \cap V = \varnothing .$$
44: 
45: Since an open set containing a point is an open neighbourhood of it
46: ([[def-neighbourhood-top]]), the condition reads: distinct points have disjoint
47: open neighbourhoods. Nothing is asserted about points that are equal, and the
48: condition is vacuous for a space with at most one point, so every such space is
49: Hausdorff.
50: 
51: **Every metrizable space is Hausdorff.** This is not proved here, because it is
52: already discharged: [[def-metrizable-space]] records it among the two things
53: every metrizable space has, deriving it from
54: [[thm-metric-hausdorff-separation]], which separates $p \ne q$ in a metric space
55: by the disjoint open balls $B(p,r)$ and $B(q,r)$ with $r = d(p,q)/2 > 0$. In
56: particular $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology, every $\mathbb{R}^n$, and every
57: subspace of a metrizable space are Hausdorff.
58: 
59: **Not every space is Hausdorff.** The indiscrete topology
60: $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{ind}} = \{\varnothing, X\}$ on a set $X = \{a,b\}$ with
61: $a \ne b$ ([[def-standard-topologies]]) is not: the only open set containing $a$
62: is $X$, the only one containing $b$ is $X$, and $X \cap X = X \ne \varnothing$.
63: This is the same two-point space that [[def-metrizable-space]] uses to exhibit a
64: topology induced by no metric, and the reason is the same one: failure of the
65: Hausdorff condition is an obstruction to metrizability.
66: 
67: **Being Hausdorff is a topological property** ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]).
68: If $h : X \to Z$ is a homeomorphism and $X$ is Hausdorff, then for $z \ne z'$ in
69: $Z$ the points $h^{-1}(z)$ and $h^{-1}(z')$ are distinct, so they have disjoint
70: open $U, V$; the images $h[U]$ and $h[V]$ are open, disjoint, and contain $z$ and
71: $z'$ respectively, a homeomorphism carrying the open sets of one space
72: bijectively onto those of the other. So no space homeomorphic to a Hausdorff
73: space fails the condition.
74: 
75: **Scope of this item.** Only the definition, the metrizable case and the
76: two-point failure are recorded here, because that is all this page uses. The
77: Hausdorff condition is one of a graded family of separation axioms; that family,
78: its ordering, and the questions of which of its members are hereditary or
79: preserved by products, are not available at this point in the reading order and
80: nothing here anticipates them. What this page does use is a single negative
81: result: a quotient of a Hausdorff space need not be Hausdorff, which is recorded
82: below as a false statement and witnessed on the companion page.
83: 
FILE items/def-hereditary-property.md
35: ## Definition
36: 
37: A **property of topological spaces** is a condition $P$ that is either true or
38: false of each space, as in [[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]; a **topological
39: property** is one whose truth value is the same for homeomorphic spaces. Every
40: subset of a space is regarded as a space by giving it the subspace topology
41: ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
42: 
43: Let $P$ be a property of topological spaces. Then $P$ is
44: 
45: - **hereditary** if, whenever a space $X$ has $P$, every subspace of $X$ has $P$;
46: - **open-hereditary** if, whenever $X$ has $P$, every subspace $S \subseteq X$
47:   with $S$ open in $X$ has $P$;
48: - **closed-hereditary** if, whenever $X$ has $P$, every subspace $S \subseteq X$
49:   with $S$ closed in $X$ has $P$.
50: 
51: A hereditary property is both open-hereditary and closed-hereditary, since the
52: condition on $S$ is only a restriction of the range of subspaces quantified over.
53: Neither of the two weaker notions implies the other, and neither implies
54: heredity.
55: 
56: **The definition is stable under the route by which a subspace is reached.** If
57: $S \subseteq T \subseteq X$ then the topology $S$ inherits from the subspace $T$
58: is the topology $S$ inherits from $X$, transitivity being discharged in
59: [[def-subspace-topology-top]]. So "every subspace of $X$" is unambiguous, and a
60: hereditary property automatically passes from $X$ to a subspace of a subspace,
61: with no separate induction.
62: 
63: **Heredity is a statement about a property, not about a space.** It quantifies
64: over all spaces having $P$ and all their subspaces, so a single space whose
65: subspaces all inherit $P$ says nothing; and a single space that **has** $P$ and
66: has one subspace lacking $P$ refutes heredity outright. A space that lacks $P$
67: refutes nothing, however its subspaces behave. That asymmetry is why the failures are
68: recorded here as counterexamples and the successes as theorems.
69: 
70: **Only topological properties are worth asking about.** Taking $S = X$ shows that
71: a hereditary property holds of $X$ itself, and the subspace topology on $X$ is
72: $\mathcal{T}$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]], with $U \cap X = U$), so the
73: definition is not vacuous at the top. But a condition that is not invariant under
74: homeomorphism can be hereditary for uninteresting reasons, since a subspace is
75: only determined up to the identification of its topology
76: ([[def-topological-space]]); every property named hereditary in this library is
77: a topological property, and it is said so where it is proved.
78: 
FILE items/def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps.md
36: ## Definition
37: 
38: Let $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ and $(Y, \mathcal{T}_Y)$ be topological spaces and let
39: $f : X \to Y$ be a function. Continuity is as in [[def-continuous-map-top]],
40: injections, surjections and bijections as in
41: [[def-injection-surjection-bijection]].
42: 
43: - $f$ is an **open map** if $f[U]$ is open in $Y$ for every open $U \subseteq X$.
44: - $f$ is a **closed map** if $f[F]$ is closed in $Y$ for every closed
45:   $F \subseteq X$.
46: - $f$ is a **homeomorphism** if $f$ is a continuous bijection whose inverse
47:   $f^{-1} : Y \to X$ is also continuous. The spaces are **homeomorphic**, written
48:   $X \cong Y$, when a homeomorphism $X \to Y$ exists.
49: - $f$ is an **embedding** if $f$ is injective and the corestriction
50:   $f_0 : X \to f[X]$, $f_0(x) = f(x)$, is a homeomorphism onto $f[X]$ carrying
51:   the subspace topology inherited from $Y$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
52: 
53: **The inverse in the third clause exists because $f$ is a bijection**, and it is
54: the unique two-sided inverse ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]); no choice
55: principle is involved. Continuity of $f^{-1}$ is a genuine additional demand: a
56: continuous bijection need not be a homeomorphism, and this page records that
57: failure as a false statement with a two-point witness.
58: 
59: **Open, closed and homeomorphism are three different conditions.** A
60: homeomorphism is continuous by definition, but an open map need not be continuous
61: and a closed map need not be continuous; and continuity implies neither openness
62: nor closedness. An open map need
63: not be closed and a closed map need not be open, and Sierpinski space
64: $S = \{a,b\}$ with open point $b$ ([[def-standard-topologies]]) witnesses both
65: failures at once: the constant map $S \to S$ with value $b$ is open, since the
66: image of every nonempty set is the open set $\{b\}$, and is not closed, since the
67: image of the closed set $\{a\}$ is $\{b\}$, whose complement $\{a\}$ is not open;
68: the constant map with value $a$ is closed and not open by the same computation
69: read the other way. What is true
70: is that for a **continuous bijection** the three notions collapse: it is a
71: homeomorphism exactly when it is open, exactly when it is closed. That is proved
72: in the next item and is not assumed here.
73: 
74: **Topological properties.** A **property of topological spaces** is a condition
75: $P$ that is either true or false of each space. $P$ is a **topological property**
76: (one is also said to be *preserved by homeomorphism*, or *invariant*) when
77: $X \cong Y$ implies that $P(X)$ and $P(Y)$ have the same truth value. Since
78: $\cong$ is an equivalence relation on spaces — the identity is a homeomorphism,
79: inverses and composites of homeomorphisms are homeomorphisms, all three verified
80: in the next item — a topological property is exactly one that is constant on each
81: $\cong$-class.
82: 
83: **What a homeomorphism transports.** If $h : X \to Y$ is a homeomorphism then
84: $U \mapsto h[U]$ is a bijection from $\mathcal{T}_X$ onto $\mathcal{T}_Y$, with
85: inverse $V \mapsto h^{-1}[V]$: both maps are well defined because $h$ and
86: $h^{-1}$ are continuous, and they are mutually inverse because $h$ is a
87: bijection. So a homeomorphism is an isomorphism of the structure "a set together
88: with a distinguished family of subsets", and every notion defined from the open
89: sets alone — closed, closure, interior, boundary, dense, convergence of
90: sequences, continuity of maps into and out of the space — is carried across by
91: it. Anything defined from extra data, such as a metric or an order, is not, and
92: that distinction is exactly what the phrase *topological property* is for.
93: 
FILE items/def-interior-closure-boundary-top.md
36: ## Definition
37: 
38: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), let
39: $A \subseteq X$ and let $x \in X$. Neighbourhoods are as in
40: [[def-neighbourhood-top]], so a neighbourhood need not be open.
41: 
42: - The **interior** of $A$ is
43:   $\operatorname{int}(A) := \bigcup \{\, U \in \mathcal{T} : U \subseteq A \,\}$.
44: - The **closure** of $A$ is
45:   $\overline{A} := \bigcap \{\, F \subseteq X : F \text{ closed and } A \subseteq F \,\}$.
46: - The **exterior** of $A$ is $\operatorname{ext}(A) := \operatorname{int}(X \setminus A)$.
47: - The **boundary** of $A$ is $\partial A := \overline{A} \setminus \operatorname{int}(A)$.
48: - $x$ is a **limit point** (accumulation point) of $A$ if every neighbourhood $N$
49:   of $x$ satisfies $N \cap (A \setminus \{x\}) \ne \varnothing$. The set of limit
50:   points of $A$ is the **derived set** $A'$.
51: - $x$ is an **isolated point of $A$** if $x \in A$ and some neighbourhood $N$ of
52:   $x$ satisfies $N \cap A = \{x\}$.
53: 
54: **Both operators are well posed, and the two names are justified rather than
55: asserted.** The interior is a union of open sets, hence open by (T2), it is
56: contained in $A$, and it contains every open $U \subseteq A$: so
57: $\operatorname{int}(A)$ is *the largest open subset of $A$*. The family being
58: intersected in the definition of $\overline{A}$ is nonempty, since $X$ is closed
59: and contains $A$, so the intersection is a set; it is closed by (C2) of
60: [[def-topological-space]], it contains $A$, and it is contained in every closed
61: $F \supseteq A$: so $\overline{A}$ is *the smallest closed superset of $A$*. In
62: particular
63: 
64: $$\operatorname{int}(A) \subseteq A \subseteq \overline{A},$$
65: 
66: and $A$ is open if and only if $A = \operatorname{int}(A)$, and closed if and
67: only if $A = \overline{A}$, in each case because one inclusion is automatic and
68: the other says exactly that $A$ belongs to the family in question.
69: 
70: **Interior and closure are exchanged by complementation.** For every
71: $A \subseteq X$,
72: 
73: $$X \setminus \operatorname{int}(A) = \overline{X \setminus A}, \qquad X \setminus \overline{A} = \operatorname{int}(X \setminus A) = \operatorname{ext}(A).$$
74: 
75: Indeed $U \mapsto X \setminus U$ is a bijection between the open subsets of $A$
76: and the closed supersets of $X \setminus A$, and it turns unions into
77: intersections ([[def-topological-space]]); applying complementation to the first
78: identity gives the second. So $\operatorname{ext}(A) = X \setminus \overline{A}$,
79: and every statement about interiors has a mirror statement about closures.
80: 
81: **The pointwise description of the interior.** $x \in \operatorname{int}(A)$ if
82: and only if $A$ is a neighbourhood of $x$. If $x \in \operatorname{int}(A)$ then
83: $\operatorname{int}(A)$ is an open set with $x \in \operatorname{int}(A) \subseteq A$;
84: conversely a neighbourhood relation $x \in U \subseteq A$ with $U$ open puts $x$
85: in the union defining $\operatorname{int}(A)$. The corresponding description of
86: the closure is proved as the next item, because it is the statement that does the
87: work in every later proof.
88: 
89: **$X$ is the disjoint union of the three regions.** Since
90: $\operatorname{int}(A) \subseteq \overline{A}$, the three sets
91: $\operatorname{int}(A)$, $\partial A = \overline{A} \setminus \operatorname{int}(A)$
92: and $\operatorname{ext}(A) = X \setminus \overline{A}$ are pairwise disjoint and
93: their union is $X$. This is recorded again, with the identities for interior and
94: closure of unions and intersections, in the lemma two items below.
95: 
FILE items/def-interval.md
29: ## Definition
30: 
31: Throughout, $\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field
32: ([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]) with its order
33: ([[def-real-order]]).
34: 
35: A subset $I \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is **order-convex** when
36: 
37: $$x, y \in I \text{ and } x \le z \le y \;\Longrightarrow\; z \in I .$$
38: 
39: The **intervals** of $\mathbb{R}$ are the sets of the following nine forms, where
40: $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$:
41: 
42: | | bounded forms | | one-sided and full forms |
43: |---|---|---|---|
44: | $(a,b)$ | $\{x : a < x < b\}$ | $(a,\infty)$ | $\{x : a < x\}$ |
45: | $[a,b]$ | $\{x : a \le x \le b\}$ | $[a,\infty)$ | $\{x : a \le x\}$ |
46: | $[a,b)$ | $\{x : a \le x < b\}$ | $(-\infty,b)$ | $\{x : x < b\}$ |
47: | $(a,b]$ | $\{x : a < x \le b\}$ | $(-\infty,b]$ | $\{x : x \le b\}$ |
48: | | | $(-\infty,\infty)$ | $\mathbb{R}$ |
49: 
50: An interval is **open** when both of its written endpoints are excluded, that is
51: for the forms $(a,b)$, $(a,\infty)$, $(-\infty,b)$ and $(-\infty,\infty)$; it is
52: **closed** when both written endpoints are included, that is for $[a,b]$,
53: $[a,\infty)$, $(-\infty,b]$ and $(-\infty,\infty)$. The forms $[a,b)$ and
54: $(a,b]$ are **half-open**.
55: 
56: **The symbols $\pm\infty$ are notation and not elements of $\mathbb{R}$.** They
57: mark which side carries no endpoint condition at all; the five forms in the right
58: column are defined by the displayed conditions on $x$ alone, and no arithmetic is
59: ever performed with $\infty$. This is the same refusal to extend $\mathbb{R}$
60: silently that [[rem-sup-conventions]] records for suprema.
61: 
62: **Every one of the nine forms is order-convex.** Each is defined by a conjunction
63: of at most two conditions, each of the shape $a < x$, $a \le x$, $x < b$ or
64: $x \le b$, and each such condition is inherited by an intermediate point: if
65: $a < x$ and $x \le z$ then $a < z$, and if $z \le y$ and $y < b$ then $z < b$,
66: by transitivity of the order ([[def-ordered-field]]). Applying this to whichever
67: one or two conditions define the form in question gives $z \in I$ whenever
68: $x, y \in I$ and $x \le z \le y$.
69: 
70: **Bounded intervals.** An interval is **bounded** ([[def-bounded-set]]) exactly
71: when it is of one of the four forms in the left column: for those, $a$ is a lower
72: bound and $b$ an upper bound. The other five forms are unbounded, on the side or
73: sides written with $\infty$; the verification is in the remarks below.
74: 
75: **Nondegeneracy.** An interval is **degenerate** when it has at most one element,
76: and **nondegenerate** when it has at least two. For the four bounded forms with
77: endpoints $a$ and $b$:
78: 
79: - $[a,b]$ is nonempty exactly when $a \le b$, and it is nondegenerate exactly
80:   when $a < b$. It is the singleton $\{a\}$ when $a = b$.
81: - $(a,b)$, $[a,b)$ and $(a,b]$ are nonempty exactly when $a < b$, and then each
82:   is nondegenerate.
83: 
84: The only assertion here that is not immediate from the defining conditions is
85: that $a < b$ makes $(a,b)$ nonempty with at least two points. It holds because
86: $a < (a+b)/2 < b$, which follows from $a < b$ by adding $a$, respectively $b$, to
87: both sides and halving ([[def-ordered-field]]); repeating the halving inside
88: $(a, (a+b)/2)$ produces a second point.
89: 
90: **Closed bounded intervals.** These are the sets $[a,b]$ with $a \le b$, which is
91: exactly the condition making them nonempty. They are the intervals the nested
92: interval property is stated for, and the phrase *closed bounded interval* always
93: carries the hypothesis $a \le b$ in this library.
94: 
95: **Length.** The **length** of a bounded interval presented by its endpoints
96: $a \le b$ is
97: 
98: $$\ell = b - a \;\ge\; 0 .$$
99: 
100: Length is attached to the presentation by endpoints and is not recovered from the
101: set: $(a,b)$, $[a,b)$ and $(a,b]$ are all empty when $a = b$, and so is
102: $(c,c)$ for any other $c$, while each of these presentations has length $0$, so
103: nothing inconsistent arises; but the endpoints are named explicitly at every
104: point where a length is used in this library, and never inferred from the set.
105: Unbounded intervals are assigned no length.
106: 
FILE items/def-locally-compact-space.md
41: ## Definition
42: 
43: A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$ ([[def-topological-space]]) is **locally
44: compact** when
45: 
46: > every point of $X$ has a compact neighbourhood:
47: 
48: that is, for every $x \in X$ there is a neighbourhood $N$ of $x$
49: ([[def-neighbourhood-top]]) that is a compact subset of $X$
50: ([[def-compact-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
51: 
52: **A neighbourhood need not be open here**, and that is what makes the condition
53: the weak one it is meant to be: $N$ is required only to contain some open set
54: containing $x$. Writing "compact **open** neighbourhood" instead would define a
55: strictly stronger property, satisfied by no space in which a point has no compact
56: open neighbourhood, $\mathbb{R}$ among them; and requiring the compact set merely
57: to *contain* $x$ would define a property so weak that every space with a
58: singleton has it, singletons being compact.
59: 
60: **Every compact space is locally compact**, since $X$ itself is a neighbourhood
61: of each of its points and is a compact subset of itself. The converse fails, and
62: $\mathbb{R}^n$ is the standard witness.
63: 
64: **What the condition says in a metric space.** Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space
65: carrying its metric topology ([[def-metric-topology]], [[def-metrizable-space]]),
66: with balls as in [[def-metric-ball]], and let $x \in X$. Then
67: 
68: > $x$ has a compact neighbourhood **if and only if** there are a real $r > 0$ and
69: > a compact $K \subseteq X$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq K$.
70: 
71: *Both directions are immediate and are discharged here.* If $N$ is a compact
72: neighbourhood of $x$, fix an open $U$ with $x \in U \subseteq N$; by
73: [[def-metric-topology]] there is $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq U \subseteq N$, so
74: $K := N$ serves. Conversely, if $B(x,r) \subseteq K$ with $K$ compact, then $K$
75: contains the open set $B(x,r)$, which contains $x$, so $K$ is a neighbourhood of
76: $x$ and is compact. Compactness of a subset of $(X,d)$ means the same thing read
77: metrically and read topologically
78: ([[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]]), so the criterion may be
79: applied with either development's theorems.
80: 
81: **$\mathbb{R}^n$ is locally compact for every $n \ge 1$.** Give $\mathbb{R}^n$
82: the product topology, which is the metric topology of $d_\infty$
83: ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]], [[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]]). For
84: $p \in \mathbb{R}^n$ the set
85: 
86: $$Q_p \;:=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^n : d_\infty(x,p) \le 1 \,\}$$
87: 
88: is closed, being the complement of the union of the open balls $B(y, d_\infty(y,p) - 1)$
89: over the points $y$ with $d_\infty(y,p) > 1$, and it is bounded
90: ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]), lying inside $B(p, 2)$; so $Q_p$ is compact by
91: [[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]]. It contains the open ball $B(p,1)$,
92: which contains $p$, so it is a compact neighbourhood of $p$. The space
93: $\mathbb{R}^n$ is not compact, so local compactness is strictly weaker than
94: compactness.
95: 
FILE items/def-metric-ball.md
37: ## Definition
38: 
39: Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), let $x \in X$ and let
40: $r \in \mathbb{R}$ with $r > 0$ ([[def-real-order]]). Define
41: 
42: $$B(x,r) := \{\, y \in X : d(x,y) < r \,\}, \qquad \bar B(x,r) := \{\, y \in X : d(x,y) \le r \,\}, \qquad S(x,r) := \{\, y \in X : d(x,y) = r \,\}.$$
43: 
44: $B(x,r)$ is the **open ball**, $\bar B(x,r)$ the **closed ball** and $S(x,r)$ the
45: **sphere** of **centre** $x$ and **radius** $r$. The radius is always a strictly
46: positive real; a ball of radius $0$ or of negative radius is never written in
47: this library.
48: 
49: **Immediate consequences of the definitions.** For every $x \in X$ and $r > 0$:
50: 
51: - $x \in B(x,r)$, because $d(x,x) = 0 < r$ (axiom (M1) of [[def-metric-space]]);
52:   in particular open and closed balls are nonempty.
53: - $B(x,r) \subseteq \bar B(x,r)$ and $S(x,r) \subseteq \bar B(x,r)$, and
54:   $\bar B(x,r)$ is the disjoint union of $B(x,r)$ and $S(x,r)$, by trichotomy of
55:   the order of $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]):
56:   each $y$ satisfies exactly one of $d(x,y) < r$, $d(x,y) = r$, $d(x,y) > r$.
57: - If $0 < s \le r$ then $B(x,s) \subseteq B(x,r)$ and
58:   $\bar B(x,s) \subseteq \bar B(x,r)$, by transitivity of the order.
59: - Nonnegativity of the metric ([[lem-metric-nonnegativity]]) is what forces the
60:   radius convention, and it forces it for the **open** ball only: if $r \le 0$
61:   then $B(x,r) = \{y : d(x,y) < r\}$ is empty, because $d(x,y) \ge 0 \ge r$
62:   for every $y$. The other two sets behave differently at $r = 0$, and the
63:   convention $r > 0$ excludes them for uniformity rather than for emptiness:
64:   $\bar B(x,0) = S(x,0) = \{x\}$, since $d(x,y) \le 0$ together with
65:   $d(x,y) \ge 0$ gives $d(x,y) = 0$ and hence $y = x$ by (M1). For $r < 0$ all
66:   three sets are empty.
67: 
68: **A sphere may be empty, and so the three sets are not on a par.** For $r > 0$
69: the open and closed balls always contain $x$, but nothing in the definition
70: produces a point at distance exactly $r$ from $x$. If a metric takes only the
71: values $0$ and $1$, as the discrete metric on the companion page does, then
72: $S(x,2) = \emptyset$ while $B(x,2) = \bar B(x,2)$ is the whole space. So
73: nonemptiness of a sphere is never available by convention: where it is used, it
74: is proved.
75: 
76: **The ambient space is part of the notation.** $B(x,r)$ depends on $(X,d)$ and
77: not on $x$ and $r$ alone. When more than one space or more than one metric is in
78: play we write $B_X(x,r)$, or $B_d(x,r)$, and likewise for $\bar B$ and $S$. This
79: matters as soon as subspaces appear ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]): a
80: ball of a subspace is the trace on it of a ball of the ambient space, and the two
81: are different sets.
82: 
FILE items/def-metric-bounded-diameter.md
42: ## Definition
43: 
44: Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let
45: $A, B \subseteq X$.
46: 
47: **Bounded subset.** $A$ is **bounded** if $A = \emptyset$ or there are $x_0 \in X$
48: and a real $r > 0$ with $A \subseteq B(x_0, r)$ ([[def-metric-ball]]). The space
49: $(X,d)$ is a **bounded metric space** if $X$ is a bounded subset of itself.
50: 
51: **Diameter, for nonempty bounded $A$ only.** Suppose $A$ is nonempty and
52: bounded, and put
53: 
54: $$D(A) := \{\, d(a,b) : a, b \in A \,\} \subseteq \mathbb{R}.$$
55: 
56: Then $D(A)$ is nonempty, since $A$ is, and it is bounded above: fixing $x_0$ and
57: $r$ with $A \subseteq B(x_0,r)$, every $a, b \in A$ satisfy
58: $d(a,b) \le d(a,x_0) + d(x_0,b) < r + r$ by the triangle inequality, symmetry
59: ([[def-metric-space]]) and addition of inequalities ([[lem-of-add-order]],
60: [[def-ordered-field]]), so $r + r$ is an upper bound of $D(A)$
61: ([[def-bounded-set]]). Hence $D(A)$ has a least upper bound in $\mathbb{R}$ by
62: the least-upper-bound property ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]), and that bound
63: is unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]). Define
64: 
65: $$\operatorname{diam}(A) := \sup D(A).$$
66: 
67: **Distance from a point to a set, for nonempty $A$ only.** Let $x \in X$ and let
68: $A$ be nonempty, and put $E(x,A) := \{\, d(x,a) : a \in A \,\}$. Then $E(x,A)$ is
69: nonempty and bounded below by $0$, since a metric is nonnegative
70: ([[lem-metric-nonnegativity]], [[def-bounded-set]]), so it has a greatest lower
71: bound ([[thm-infimum-property]], [[def-infimum]]), unique by [[lem-sup-unique]].
72: Define
73: 
74: $$d(x,A) := \inf E(x,A).$$
75: 
76: **Distance between two sets, for nonempty $A$ and $B$ only.** Put
77: $E(A,B) := \{\, d(a,b) : a \in A,\ b \in B \,\}$, again nonempty and bounded
78: below by $0$, and define
79: 
80: $$d(A,B) := \inf E(A,B).$$
81: 
82: **Every one of the three scope restrictions is load bearing.** In this library
83: $\sup$ and $\inf$ denote real numbers and are written only after existence has
84: been established; the extended real line is introduced on a later page and is
85: not used for the suprema and infima taken here, and no convention
86: $\sup \emptyset = -\infty$ is in force in this development
87: ([[rem-sup-conventions]]). Accordingly:
88: 
89: - $\operatorname{diam}(A)$ is defined exactly when $A$ is nonempty **and**
90:   bounded. It is not defined for $A = \emptyset$, and it is not defined, not even
91:   as an infinite value, for an unbounded $A$.
92: - $d(x,A)$ is defined exactly when $A \ne \emptyset$, and $d(A,B)$ exactly when
93:   both $A$ and $B$ are nonempty. No boundedness is needed for these two, because
94:   $0$ is always a lower bound.
95: 
FILE items/def-metric-topology.md
37: ## Definition
38: 
39: Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]).
40: 
41: A subset $U \subseteq X$ is **open in $(X,d)$** if for every $x \in U$ there is a
42: real $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq U$ ([[def-metric-ball]]). A subset
43: $F \subseteq X$ is **closed in $(X,d)$** if its complement $X \setminus F$ is
44: open.
45: 
46: The collection
47: 
48: $$\mathcal{T}_d := \{\, U \subseteq X : U \text{ is open in } (X,d) \,\}$$
49: 
50: of all open subsets is the **metric topology** of $d$ on $X$. A subset of $X$
51: that is both open and closed is called **clopen**.
52: 
53: **Two sets are open for trivial reasons.** $\emptyset$ is open, because the
54: defining condition quantifies over no points; and $X$ is open, because
55: $B(x,r) \subseteq X$ for every $x$ and every $r > 0$. Consequently $X$ and
56: $\emptyset$ are also closed, and both are clopen.
57: 
58: **A neighbourhood of a point $x$** is any open set containing $x$. The condition
59: above therefore reads: $U$ is open exactly when every point of $U$ has a ball
60: around it inside $U$, and it is the balls alone that have to be tested.
61: 
62: **The metric, not the set, determines $\mathcal{T}_d$.** Two metrics on the same
63: set may have different metric topologies, and two different metrics may have the
64: same one; the systematic comparison is [[def-equivalent-metrics]].
65: 
FILE items/def-metrizable-space.md
48: ## Definition
49: 
50: A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$ ([[def-topological-space]]) is
51: **metrizable** if there is a metric $d$ on $X$ ([[def-metric-space]]) whose
52: metric topology is $\mathcal{T}$, that is $\mathcal{T} = \mathcal{T}_d$
53: ([[def-metric-topology]]). Such a $d$ is said to **induce** or **metrise**
54: $\mathcal{T}$.
55: 
56: **The definition presupposes that $\mathcal{T}_d$ is a topology in the sense of
57: [[def-topological-space]], and it is.** By [[def-metric-topology]] both
58: $\varnothing$ and $X$ are open in $(X,d)$, which is (T1), and by
59: [[thm-metric-open-set-algebra]] the family $\mathcal{T}_d$ is closed under
60: arbitrary unions, which is (T2), and under intersections of $n \ge 1$ members,
61: which contains (T3). So every metric space is a topological space, and the
62: metric-space development of this library is a special case of the present one.
63: 
64: **The standard local notions in the two developments agree after translating
65: their neighbourhood conventions.** Let $d$ be a metric on $X$ and give $X$ the
66: topology $\mathcal{T}_d$.
67: 
68: - *Neighbourhoods and balls.* [[def-metric-topology]] uses "neighbourhood" for
69:   an open set containing $x$, whereas [[def-neighbourhood-top]] also allows a
70:   non-open superset of such a set. Thus the two collections are not literally
71:   equal, but the open metric neighbourhoods are cofinal in the broader
72:   neighbourhood filter. The balls $B(x,r)$, $r > 0$ ([[def-metric-ball]]), are
73:   open ([[thm-metric-open-set-algebra]]) and form a neighbourhood base at $x$:
74:   any neighbourhood $N$ contains an open $U \ni x$, hence a ball around $x$ by
75:   [[def-metric-topology]]. The balls of radius $1/n$ alone already suffice
76:   ([[lem-metric-ball-neighbourhood-base]]).
77: - *Interior, closure, boundary.* [[def-metric-interior-closure-boundary]] defines
78:   them by the same conditions with balls in place of neighbourhoods, and the
79:   previous bullet makes the two conditions equivalent; the metric closure is the
80:   smallest closed superset ([[thm-metric-closure-characterisation]]), which is the
81:   definition used here ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]). So the two
82:   closures, the two interiors and the two boundaries are the same three
83:   operations.
84: - *Convergence.* $x_k \to p$ in the sense of [[def-sequence-convergence-top]] is
85:   "eventually in every neighbourhood of $p$", and by the first bullet this is
86:   "eventually in every ball around $p$", which is [[def-metric-convergence]].
87: - *Continuity.* $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ continuity at $a$
88:   ([[def-metric-continuity]]) says that every ball around $f(a)$ has a ball
89:   around $a$ mapped into it, which by the first bullet is continuity at $a$ in
90:   the sense of [[def-continuous-map-top]].
91: 
92: - *Subspaces.* For $A \subseteq X$ the subspace topology
93:   $\{\, U \cap A : U \in \mathcal{T}_d \,\}$ of [[def-subspace-topology-top]] is
94:   exactly the metric topology of the subspace metric $d_A$
95:   ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]), so the two uses of the phrase
96:   *subspace topology* in this library name one thing. Indeed
97:   $B_A(a,r) = B_X(a,r) \cap A$: a trace $U \cap A$ is $d_A$-open, since each of
98:   its points $a$ has $B_X(a,r) \subseteq U$ and hence $B_A(a,r) \subseteq U \cap A$;
99:   and a $d_A$-open $W$ is the trace of
100:   $U := \bigcup \{\, B_X(a,r) : a \in W,\ r > 0,\ B_X(a,r) \cap A \subseteq W \,\}$,
101:   which is $d_X$-open, satisfies $U \cap A = W$, and involves no choice principle,
102:   the union being taken over a set of pairs rather than over a selection.
103: 
104: Consequently the metric-space notions of interior, closure, boundary, density,
105: convergence, continuity and subspace agree with the topological notions here,
106: and statements about them transfer once a metric is named. For neighbourhoods
107: the transfer uses the explicit convention change above: a metric-page
108: neighbourhood is an open topological neighbourhood, while every topological
109: neighbourhood contains one.
110: 
111: **Metrizability is a topological property; the metric is not part of it.** If
112: $h : X \to Y$ is a homeomorphism ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]) and $d$
113: metrises $X$, then $d'(y_1,y_2) := d(h^{-1}(y_1), h^{-1}(y_2))$ is a metric on
114: $Y$ — the three axioms transfer along the bijection $h^{-1}$ — and
115: $h[B_d(x,r)] = B_{d'}(h(x), r)$, so $h$ carries a basis of $\mathcal{T}_d$ to a
116: basis of $\mathcal{T}_{d'}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{d'} = h[\mathcal{T}_d] = \mathcal{T}_Y$.
117: Hence $Y$ is metrizable. The metric itself, however, is **not** determined by the
118: topology: two metrics on one set may induce the same topology without agreeing,
119: which is exactly topological equivalence ([[def-equivalent-metrics]]), and
120: properties of a metric that are not properties of its topology, boundedness among
121: them, are therefore not properties of a metrizable space.
122: 
123: **Two things every metrizable space has.** It is **Hausdorff**: distinct points
124: have disjoint open neighbourhoods, by [[thm-metric-hausdorff-separation]] applied
125: to any metric inducing the topology. And it is **first countable**
126: ([[def-first-countable-top]]), by [[lem-metric-ball-neighbourhood-base]]. Either
127: failure is therefore an obstruction to metrizability, and this page uses the
128: first of them to exhibit a topology induced by no metric.
129: 
130: **Sequential limits in a metrizable space are unique, so the notation
131: $\lim_k x_k$ is available there.** In a metric space a sequence has at most one
132: limit ([[lem-metric-limits-unique]]), and by the agreement of convergence above
133: that uniqueness is a statement about the topology alone; so within a metrizable
134: space, and only there, this page writes $\lim_k x_k$ in the ordinary way. In a
135: general space the symbol is unavailable ([[def-sequence-convergence-top]]).
136: 
137: **The usual topology of $\mathbb{R}$.** The absolute value makes $\mathbb{R}$ a
138: metric space under $d_{\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$, its open balls are the bounded
139: open intervals, and the resulting metric topology is what claim 3 of
140: [[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]] names the **usual topology** of $\mathbb{R}$.
141: That is the topology meant by the phrase throughout these two pages, and
142: $\mathbb{R}$ carrying it is metrizable by definition. Every statement about it on
143: these two pages is proved from the metric $d_{\mathbb{R}}$ and the bridge above,
144: and no example re-derives any of it.
145: 
FILE items/def-natural-numbers.md
31: ## Definition
32: 
33: The set of **natural numbers** is the **smallest inductive set**
34: ([[def-inductive-set]]),
35: 
36: $$\mathbb{N} = \omega := \bigcap\,\{\, I : I \text{ is inductive} \,\},$$
37: 
38: which exists and is itself inductive by [[lem-omega-smallest-inductive]] (the
39: Axiom of Infinity supplies one inductive set to intersect within, and Separation
40: makes the intersection a set). On $\mathbb{N}$ we take
41: 
42: $$0 := \varnothing, \qquad \sigma(n) := n \cup \{n\},$$
43: 
44: the distinguished element and the successor function. Thus
45: $0 = \varnothing$, $1 = \{0\}$, $2 = \{0, 1\}$, $3 = \{0, 1, 2\}$, and in general
46: $n = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ is the set of its predecessors.
47: 
FILE items/def-neighbourhood-top.md
42: ## Definition
43: 
44: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and let
45: $x \in X$.
46: 
47: A set $N \subseteq X$ is a **neighbourhood of $x$** if there is an open
48: $U \in \mathcal{T}$ with $x \in U \subseteq N$. The family of all neighbourhoods
49: of $x$ is written $\mathcal{N}(x)$ and called the **neighbourhood filter** at
50: $x$. A neighbourhood that is itself open is an **open neighbourhood**.
51: 
52: **Convention, and it is a live fork: in this library a neighbourhood need not be
53: open.** The competing convention, used by Munkres among others, defines a
54: neighbourhood of $x$ to be an *open* set containing $x$. Both are in current use;
55: this library follows the one above and writes "open neighbourhood" in full
56: whenever openness is wanted, so that no statement here depends on which
57: convention a reader brings.
58: 
59: A family $\mathcal{B}_x \subseteq \mathcal{N}(x)$ is a **neighbourhood base at
60: $x$** if every neighbourhood of $x$ contains a member of $\mathcal{B}_x$: for
61: every $N \in \mathcal{N}(x)$ there is $B \in \mathcal{B}_x$ with $B \subseteq N$.
62: 
63: **Four immediate consequences, established here because they are used
64: constantly.**
65: 
66: 1. *$X$ is a neighbourhood of each of its points*, since $x \in X \subseteq X$
67:    and $X$ is open by (T1); so $\mathcal{N}(x) \ne \varnothing$ and every point
68:    has at least one neighbourhood base, namely $\mathcal{N}(x)$ itself.
69: 2. *A superset of a neighbourhood of $x$ is a neighbourhood of $x$*: if
70:    $x \in U \subseteq N \subseteq M$ with $U$ open then $x \in U \subseteq M$.
71: 3. *The intersection of two neighbourhoods of $x$ is a neighbourhood of $x$*: if
72:    $x \in U \subseteq N$ and $x \in V \subseteq M$ with $U, V$ open, then
73:    $U \cap V$ is open by (T3) and $x \in U \cap V \subseteq N \cap M$. By
74:    iteration the same holds for any intersection of finitely many
75:    neighbourhoods of $x$.
76: 4. *A set is open exactly when it is a neighbourhood of each of its points.* If
77:    $U$ is open and $x \in U$ then $x \in U \subseteq U$. Conversely, if $N$ is a
78:    neighbourhood of each of its points, choose for each $x \in N$ an open $U_x$
79:    with $x \in U_x \subseteq N$; then $N = \bigcup_{x \in N} U_x$ is open by (T2).
80:    No choice principle is involved: $U_x$ may be taken to be the union of *all*
81:    open subsets of $N$ containing $x$, which is determined by $x$ and $N$, and is
82:    open by (T2).
83: 
84: **Basic sets give neighbourhood bases.** If $\mathcal{B}$ is a basis for
85: $\mathcal{T}$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]) then
86: $\mathcal{B}_x := \{\, B \in \mathcal{B} : x \in B \,\}$ is a neighbourhood base
87: at $x$ consisting of open sets. Each such $B$ is open and contains $x$, hence is
88: a neighbourhood of $x$; and if $N \in \mathcal{N}(x)$, fix open $U$ with
89: $x \in U \subseteq N$ and then $B \in \mathcal{B}$ with $x \in B \subseteq U$,
90: which gives $B \in \mathcal{B}_x$ with $B \subseteq N$. A member of
91: $\mathcal{B}_x$ is called a **basic neighbourhood** of $x$.
92: 

