    35	## Definition
    36	
    37	A **property of topological spaces** is a condition $P$ that is either true or
    38	false of each space, as in [[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]; a **topological
    39	property** is one whose truth value is the same for homeomorphic spaces. Every
    40	subset of a space is regarded as a space by giving it the subspace topology
    41	([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
    42	
    43	Let $P$ be a property of topological spaces. Then $P$ is
    44	
    45	- **hereditary** if, whenever a space $X$ has $P$, every subspace of $X$ has $P$;
    46	- **open-hereditary** if, whenever $X$ has $P$, every subspace $S \subseteq X$
    47	  with $S$ open in $X$ has $P$;
    48	- **closed-hereditary** if, whenever $X$ has $P$, every subspace $S \subseteq X$
    49	  with $S$ closed in $X$ has $P$.
    50	
    51	A hereditary property is both open-hereditary and closed-hereditary, since the
    52	condition on $S$ is only a restriction of the range of subspaces quantified over.
    53	Neither of the two weaker notions implies the other, and neither implies
    54	heredity.
    55	
    56	**The definition is stable under the route by which a subspace is reached.** If
    57	$S \subseteq T \subseteq X$ then the topology $S$ inherits from the subspace $T$
    58	is the topology $S$ inherits from $X$, transitivity being discharged in
    59	[[def-subspace-topology-top]]. So "every subspace of $X$" is unambiguous, and a
    60	hereditary property automatically passes from $X$ to a subspace of a subspace,
    61	with no separate induction.
    62	
    63	**Heredity is a statement about a property, not about a space.** It quantifies
    64	over all spaces having $P$ and all their subspaces, so a single space whose
    65	subspaces all inherit $P$ says nothing; and a single space that **has** $P$ and
    66	has one subspace lacking $P$ refutes heredity outright. A space that lacks $P$
    67	refutes nothing, however its subspaces behave. That asymmetry is why the failures are
    68	recorded here as counterexamples and the successes as theorems.
    69	
    70	**Only topological properties are worth asking about.** Taking $S = X$ shows that
    71	a hereditary property holds of $X$ itself, and the subspace topology on $X$ is
    72	$\mathcal{T}$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]], with $U \cap X = U$), so the
    73	definition is not vacuous at the top. But a condition that is not invariant under
    74	homeomorphism can be hereditary for uninteresting reasons, since a subspace is
    75	only determined up to the identification of its topology
    76	([[def-topological-space]]); every property named hereditary in this library is
    77	a topological property, and it is said so where it is proved.
    78	
    79	## Remarks
    80	
    81	- **The three notions separate in practice.** Metrizability and first
    82	  countability are hereditary, and that is proved in the next item. "Has a
    83	  countable dense subset" is open-hereditary, by claim 4 of
    84	  [[thm-subspace-closure-and-interior]], and is *not* hereditary; the witness is
    85	  worked on the companion page, where an uncountable discrete subspace is
    86	  exhibited inside a space that has a countable dense subset.
    87	
    88	- **What is deliberately not settled here.** Whether the separation properties
    89	  beyond the Hausdorff condition of [[def-hausdorff-space]] are hereditary is a
    90	  question about axioms that are not available at this point in the reading
    91	  order, and no claim about them is made on this page.
    92	
    93	- **Products have their own word.** A property preserved by arbitrary products is
    94	  usually called *productive*, and the same three-way refinement (finite
    95	  products, countable products, arbitrary products) applies to it. No item on
    96	  this page uses that word, because the productive theorems it would organise are
    97	  not available at this point in the reading order.
    35	
    36	## Definition
    37	
    38	Let $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ and $(Y, \mathcal{T}_Y)$ be topological spaces and let
    39	$f : X \to Y$ be a function. Continuity is as in [[def-continuous-map-top]],
    40	injections, surjections and bijections as in
    41	[[def-injection-surjection-bijection]].
    42	
    43	- $f$ is an **open map** if $f[U]$ is open in $Y$ for every open $U \subseteq X$.
    44	- $f$ is a **closed map** if $f[F]$ is closed in $Y$ for every closed
    45	  $F \subseteq X$.
    46	- $f$ is a **homeomorphism** if $f$ is a continuous bijection whose inverse
    47	  $f^{-1} : Y \to X$ is also continuous. The spaces are **homeomorphic**, written
    48	  $X \cong Y$, when a homeomorphism $X \to Y$ exists.
    49	- $f$ is an **embedding** if $f$ is injective and the corestriction
    50	  $f_0 : X \to f[X]$, $f_0(x) = f(x)$, is a homeomorphism onto $f[X]$ carrying
    51	  the subspace topology inherited from $Y$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
    52	
    53	**The inverse in the third clause exists because $f$ is a bijection**, and it is
    54	the unique two-sided inverse ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]); no choice
    55	principle is involved. Continuity of $f^{-1}$ is a genuine additional demand: a
    56	continuous bijection need not be a homeomorphism, and this page records that
    57	failure as a false statement with a two-point witness.
    58	
    59	**Open, closed and homeomorphism are three different conditions.** A
    60	homeomorphism is continuous by definition, but an open map need not be continuous
    61	and a closed map need not be continuous; and continuity implies neither openness
    62	nor closedness. An open map need
    63	not be closed and a closed map need not be open, and Sierpinski space
    64	$S = \{a,b\}$ with open point $b$ ([[def-standard-topologies]]) witnesses both
    65	failures at once: the constant map $S \to S$ with value $b$ is open, since the
    66	image of every nonempty set is the open set $\{b\}$, and is not closed, since the
    67	image of the closed set $\{a\}$ is $\{b\}$, whose complement $\{a\}$ is not open;
    68	the constant map with value $a$ is closed and not open by the same computation
    69	read the other way. What is true
    70	is that for a **continuous bijection** the three notions collapse: it is a
    71	homeomorphism exactly when it is open, exactly when it is closed. That is proved
    72	in the next item and is not assumed here.
    73	
    74	**Topological properties.** A **property of topological spaces** is a condition
    75	$P$ that is either true or false of each space. $P$ is a **topological property**
    76	(one is also said to be *preserved by homeomorphism*, or *invariant*) when
    77	$X \cong Y$ implies that $P(X)$ and $P(Y)$ have the same truth value. Since
    78	$\cong$ is an equivalence relation on spaces — the identity is a homeomorphism,
    79	inverses and composites of homeomorphisms are homeomorphisms, all three verified
    80	in the next item — a topological property is exactly one that is constant on each
    81	$\cong$-class.
    82	
    83	**What a homeomorphism transports.** If $h : X \to Y$ is a homeomorphism then
    84	$U \mapsto h[U]$ is a bijection from $\mathcal{T}_X$ onto $\mathcal{T}_Y$, with
    85	inverse $V \mapsto h^{-1}[V]$: both maps are well defined because $h$ and
    86	$h^{-1}$ are continuous, and they are mutually inverse because $h$ is a
    87	bijection. So a homeomorphism is an isomorphism of the structure "a set together
    88	with a distinguished family of subsets", and every notion defined from the open
    89	sets alone — closed, closure, interior, boundary, dense, convergence of
    90	sequences, continuity of maps into and out of the space — is carried across by
    91	it. Anything defined from extra data, such as a metric or an order, is not, and
    92	that distinction is exactly what the phrase *topological property* is for.
    93	
    94	## Remarks
    95	
    96	- **Being an embedding is not the same as being injective and continuous.** The
    97	  identity from a set with the discrete topology to the same set with a coarser
    98	  topology is injective and continuous, its image is the whole space, and it is
    99	  an embedding only if the two topologies agree. The extra content of "embedding"
   100	  is that the source topology is recovered as the trace of the target one, which
   101	  is precisely the characteristic property of the subspace topology.
   102	
   103	- **The inclusion of a subspace is the model embedding.** For $S \subseteq X$
   104	  with the subspace topology, $\iota : S \to X$ is injective and its
   105	  corestriction to $\iota[S] = S$ is the identity of $(S, \mathcal{T}_S)$, hence
    35	
    36	## Definition
    37	
    38	Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), let
    39	$A \subseteq X$ and let $x \in X$. Neighbourhoods are as in
    40	[[def-neighbourhood-top]], so a neighbourhood need not be open.
    41	
    42	- The **interior** of $A$ is
    43	  $\operatorname{int}(A) := \bigcup \{\, U \in \mathcal{T} : U \subseteq A \,\}$.
    44	- The **closure** of $A$ is
    45	  $\overline{A} := \bigcap \{\, F \subseteq X : F \text{ closed and } A \subseteq F \,\}$.
    46	- The **exterior** of $A$ is $\operatorname{ext}(A) := \operatorname{int}(X \setminus A)$.
    47	- The **boundary** of $A$ is $\partial A := \overline{A} \setminus \operatorname{int}(A)$.
    48	- $x$ is a **limit point** (accumulation point) of $A$ if every neighbourhood $N$
    49	  of $x$ satisfies $N \cap (A \setminus \{x\}) \ne \varnothing$. The set of limit
    50	  points of $A$ is the **derived set** $A'$.
    51	- $x$ is an **isolated point of $A$** if $x \in A$ and some neighbourhood $N$ of
    52	  $x$ satisfies $N \cap A = \{x\}$.
    53	
    54	**Both operators are well posed, and the two names are justified rather than
    55	asserted.** The interior is a union of open sets, hence open by (T2), it is
    56	contained in $A$, and it contains every open $U \subseteq A$: so
    57	$\operatorname{int}(A)$ is *the largest open subset of $A$*. The family being
    58	intersected in the definition of $\overline{A}$ is nonempty, since $X$ is closed
    59	and contains $A$, so the intersection is a set; it is closed by (C2) of
    60	[[def-topological-space]], it contains $A$, and it is contained in every closed
    61	$F \supseteq A$: so $\overline{A}$ is *the smallest closed superset of $A$*. In
    62	particular
    63	
    64	$$\operatorname{int}(A) \subseteq A \subseteq \overline{A},$$
    65	
    66	and $A$ is open if and only if $A = \operatorname{int}(A)$, and closed if and
    67	only if $A = \overline{A}$, in each case because one inclusion is automatic and
    68	the other says exactly that $A$ belongs to the family in question.
    69	
    70	**Interior and closure are exchanged by complementation.** For every
    71	$A \subseteq X$,
    72	
    73	$$X \setminus \operatorname{int}(A) = \overline{X \setminus A}, \qquad X \setminus \overline{A} = \operatorname{int}(X \setminus A) = \operatorname{ext}(A).$$
    74	
    75	Indeed $U \mapsto X \setminus U$ is a bijection between the open subsets of $A$
    76	and the closed supersets of $X \setminus A$, and it turns unions into
    77	intersections ([[def-topological-space]]); applying complementation to the first
    78	identity gives the second. So $\operatorname{ext}(A) = X \setminus \overline{A}$,
    79	and every statement about interiors has a mirror statement about closures.
    80	
    81	**The pointwise description of the interior.** $x \in \operatorname{int}(A)$ if
    82	and only if $A$ is a neighbourhood of $x$. If $x \in \operatorname{int}(A)$ then
    83	$\operatorname{int}(A)$ is an open set with $x \in \operatorname{int}(A) \subseteq A$;
    84	conversely a neighbourhood relation $x \in U \subseteq A$ with $U$ open puts $x$
    85	in the union defining $\operatorname{int}(A)$. The corresponding description of
    86	the closure is proved as the next item, because it is the statement that does the
    87	work in every later proof.
    88	
    89	**$X$ is the disjoint union of the three regions.** Since
    90	$\operatorname{int}(A) \subseteq \overline{A}$, the three sets
    91	$\operatorname{int}(A)$, $\partial A = \overline{A} \setminus \operatorname{int}(A)$
    92	and $\operatorname{ext}(A) = X \setminus \overline{A}$ are pairwise disjoint and
    93	their union is $X$. This is recorded again, with the identities for interior and
    94	closure of unions and intersections, in the lemma two items below.
    95	
    96	## Remarks
    97	
    98	- **The boundary is symmetric in $A$ and its complement**:
    99	  $\partial A = \overline{A} \cap \overline{X \setminus A}$, because
   100	  $\overline{A} \setminus \operatorname{int}(A) = \overline{A} \cap (X \setminus \operatorname{int}(A)) = \overline{A} \cap \overline{X \setminus A}$
   101	  by the complementation identity above. Hence $\partial A = \partial(X \setminus A)$,
   102	  and $\partial A$ is closed, being an intersection of two closed sets.
   103	
   104	- **A limit point of $A$ need not lie in $A$, and a point of $A$ need not be a
   105	  limit point of $A$.** The points of $A$ that are not limit points of $A$ are
   106	  exactly its isolated points, directly from the two definitions. The relation
   107	  $\overline{A} = A \cup A'$ is a theorem, proved next, not a restatement.
   108	
   109	- **These are the metric notions when the topology is a metric topology.** For a
   110	  metric space the definitions of [[def-metric-interior-closure-boundary]] are
   111	  stated with balls in place of neighbourhoods, and the balls around $x$ are a
   112	  neighbourhood base at $x$; the identification is carried out where metrizable
   113	  spaces are defined, later on this page, and it is what allows metric examples
   114	  to be quoted here without reproof.
