FILE items/def-order-topology-on-a-linearly-ordered-set.md
42: ## Definition
43: 
44: Let $(L, \le)$ be a **linearly ordered set** ([[def-partial-order]]): a poset in
45: which any two elements are comparable. Write $<$ for the associated strict order.
46: 
47: ### Rays, intervals, and the order topology
48: 
49: For $a \in L$ the **open rays** at $a$ are
50: 
51: $$L_{<a} \;:=\; \{\, t \in L : t < a \,\}, \qquad L_{>a} \;:=\; \{\, t \in L : a < t \,\},$$
52: 
53: and $\mathcal{S}_L := \{\, L_{<a} : a \in L \,\} \cup \{\, L_{>a} : a \in L \,\}$
54: is the family of all of them. The **order topology** on $L$ is
55: 
56: $$\mathcal{T}_{<} \;:=\; \langle \mathcal{S}_L \rangle,$$
57: 
58: the topology generated by $\mathcal{S}_L$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]],
59: [[def-topological-space]]). A **linearly ordered topological space** is a
60: linearly ordered set carrying its order topology. For $a, b \in L$ write
61: 
62: $$(a,b) := \{\, t \in L : a < t < b \,\}, \quad [a,b] := \{\, t \in L : a \le t \le b \,\},$$
63: $$[a,b) := \{\, t \in L : a \le t < b \,\}, \quad (a,b] := \{\, t \in L : a < t \le b \,\},$$
64: 
65: so that $(a,b) = L_{>a} \cap L_{<b}$.
66: 
67: **A basis, and the obligation is discharged here.** By [[thm-basis-criterion]]
68: claim 2 the intersections of finitely many members of $\mathcal{S}_L$ form a
69: basis for $\mathcal{T}_{<}$. This library takes the empty intersection to be $L$,
70: so $L$ itself is among them. An intersection of finitely many rays is computed by
71: collecting the lower cuts and the upper cuts separately: since $\le$ is linear,
72: a finite nonempty set of elements of $L$ has a greatest and a least member, so
73: $L_{<a_1} \cap \dots \cap L_{<a_m} = L_{<a}$ with $a$ the least of the $a_i$, and
74: $L_{>b_1} \cap \dots \cap L_{>b_k} = L_{>b}$ with $b$ the greatest of the $b_j$.
75: Hence every finite intersection is $L$, an open ray, or an open interval
76: $(b,a) = L_{>b} \cap L_{<a}$, and
77: 
78: $$\mathcal{B}_L \;:=\; \{L\} \;\cup\; \mathcal{S}_L \;\cup\; \{\, (a,b) : a, b \in L \,\}$$
79: 
80: is a basis for $\mathcal{T}_{<}$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
81: 
82: **What a basic neighbourhood of a point looks like.** Let $x \in L$. If $x$ is
83: neither the least nor the greatest element of $L$ ([[def-maximal-element]]), then
84: some $a < x$ and some $b > x$ exist and $x \in (a,b)$; if $x$ is least, the sets
85: $L_{<b}$ with $b > x$ are the basic sets containing $x$ apart from $L$ itself;
86: if $x$ is greatest, they are the sets $L_{>a}$ with $a < x$. These three cases
87: are the only ones, and every proof below that argues at a point splits along
88: them.
89: 
90: **Order-convex sets.** A subset $C \subseteq L$ is **order-convex** when
91: 
92: $$x, z \in C \text{ and } x \le w \le z \;\Longrightarrow\; w \in C .$$
93: 
94: Every ray and every one of the four interval forms above is order-convex, by
95: transitivity of $\le$; so are $\varnothing$, every singleton, and $L$.
96: 
97: **A convention that is fixed once here.** A subset $C \subseteq L$ inherits two
98: topologies that need not agree: the subspace topology from $(L, \mathcal{T}_{<})$
99: ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]) and the order topology of the restricted order on
100: $C$. **In this library "a subspace of a linearly ordered topological space"
101: always means the subspace topology**, and the phrase "the order topology of $C$"
102: is written in full whenever the second is meant. The two do agree when $C$ is
103: order-convex, which is the only case used here: for order-convex $C$ the trace
104: $L_{<a} \cap C$ is $C$ if $a$ is above every element of $C$, is $\varnothing$ if
105: $a$ is below or equal to every element of $C$, and is otherwise the ray
106: $C_{<a}$ when $a \in C$, and $C_{<c}$ for any $c \in C$ above $a$ has the same
107: trace description; in every case the trace of a subbasic set of $L$ is a
108: subbasic set of $C$ or is $\varnothing$ or $C$, and conversely every ray of $C$
109: is such a trace. The general statement, for a subset that is not order-convex, is
110: **not asserted here**.
111: 
112: ### Order-density and the least upper bound property
113: 
114: Let $(L, \le)$ be linearly ordered.
115: 
116: - $L$ is **order-dense** (or **densely ordered**) when for all $x, y \in L$ with
117:   $x < y$ there is $z \in L$ with $x < z < y$. Equivalently, no element of $L$
118:   has an immediate successor above it: there is no pair $x < y$ with $(x,y) =
119:   \varnothing$.
120: - $L$ has the **least upper bound property** when every nonempty $S \subseteq L$
121:   that has an upper bound in $L$ has a least upper bound in $L$
122:   ([[def-upper-bound]]). A least upper bound is unique when it exists, by
123:   antisymmetry: two of them bound each other, and antisymmetry of $\le$
124:   ([[def-partial-order]]) forces them equal. We write $\sup S$ for it.
125: 
126: **A linear continuum** is a linearly ordered set with **at least two elements**
127: that is order-dense and has the least upper bound property.
128: 
129: **The two-element requirement is not decoration.** Without it the empty ordered
130: set and every one-point ordered set would qualify vacuously, and the theorems
131: about linear continua elsewhere in this library would have degenerate instances
132: whose statements say nothing. A linear continuum in the sense above is
133: automatically infinite: two elements $x < y$ produce $z_1$ strictly between them,
134: then $z_2$ strictly between $x$ and $z_1$, and so on, and each is new because the
135: order is strict.
136: 
137: **$\mathbb{R}$ is a linear continuum, and its order topology is its usual
138: topology.** The order of $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-real-order]], [[def-ordered-field]])
139: is linear; $\mathbb{R}$ has at least two elements, namely $0$ and $1$; it is
140: order-dense because $x < (x+y)/2 < y$ whenever $x < y$
141: ([[def-ordered-field]]); and it has the least upper bound property, which is
142: exactly the completeness axiom ([[def-complete-ordered-field]],
143: [[lem-sup-unique]], [[def-infimum]], [[def-bounded-set]]). Its order topology is
144: the usual topology, that is the metric topology of $d_{\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$
145: ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-metric-topology]],
146: [[def-metrizable-space]]): the ball $B(x,r)$ is the interval $(x - r, x + r)$
147: ([[def-metric-ball]], [[def-interval]], [[def-neighbourhood-r]]), which is a
148: basic set of $\mathcal{T}_{<}$, so every set open for the metric is open for the
149: order; and conversely $L_{<b}$ and $L_{>a}$ are open for the metric
150: ([[def-open-and-closed-in-r]]), so every subbasic set of $\mathcal{T}_{<}$ is
151: metric-open and $\mathcal{T}_{<} \subseteq \mathcal{T}_{d_{\mathbb{R}}}$ by
152: minimality of the generated topology ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]). The two
153: topologies therefore coincide, and **no second topology on $\mathbb{R}$ is being
154: introduced**.
155: 
FILE items/def-ordinal.md
42: ## Definition
43: 
44: A set $\alpha$ is an **ordinal** when both of the following hold.
45: 
46: 1. $\alpha$ is a **transitive set**: every element of $\alpha$ is also a subset
47:    of $\alpha$, that is $x \in \alpha \Rightarrow x \subseteq \alpha$.
48: 2. The membership relation restricted to $\alpha$, namely
49:    $\{(x, y) \in \alpha \times \alpha : x \in y\}$, is a **strict well-order**
50:    of $\alpha$ ([[def-well-order]]): it is irreflexive, transitive as a
51:    relation, trichotomous on $\alpha$, and every nonempty subset of $\alpha$ has
52:    an $\in$-least element.
53: 
54: Ordinals are written with lowercase Greek letters, and for ordinals we set
55: 
56: $$\alpha < \beta :\iff \alpha \in \beta, \qquad \alpha \le \beta :\iff (\alpha \in \beta \text{ or } \alpha = \beta).$$
57: 
58: Write $0 := \emptyset$, which is an ordinal because both clauses hold vacuously,
59: and write $\alpha^{+} := \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ for the **successor** of
60: $\alpha$.
61: 
FILE items/def-ordinal-addition.md
34: ## Definition
35: 
36: Let $\alpha$ and $\beta$ be ordinals ([[def-ordinal]]). The **sum**
37: $\alpha + \beta$ is defined by recursion on $\beta$, in the three cases of
38: [[def-limit-ordinal]]:
39: 
40: $$\alpha + 0 = \alpha, \qquad \alpha + \beta^{+} = (\alpha + \beta)^{+}, \qquad \alpha + \lambda = \bigcup\{\, \alpha + \beta : \beta \in \lambda \,\} \quad (\lambda \text{ a limit ordinal}).$$
41: 
42: That exactly one operation satisfies these three clauses, and that all its
43: values are ordinals, is [[cor-ordinal-addition-well-defined]], proved
44: immediately above. The union in the limit clause is the least upper bound of the
45: values already produced (claim (e) of [[lem-ordinal-basics]]), so it may be
46: written $\sup$ and the clause read as "at a limit, take the supremum".
47: 
48: **Notation.** We write $1 = 0^{+}$, $2 = 1^{+}$, and so on for the finite
49: ordinals, and $\sup$ for $\bigcup$ applied to a set of ordinals. The successor
50: operation is now a special case of addition:
51: 
52: $$\alpha + 1 = \alpha + 0^{+} = (\alpha + 0)^{+} = \alpha^{+},$$
53: 
54: so from here on $\alpha + 1$ and $\alpha^{+}$ denote the same ordinal, and both
55: notations are used, whichever reads better.
56: 
FILE items/def-product-topology.md
37: ## Definition
38: 
39: **The product set.** Let $I$ be a set and let $X_i$ be a set for each $i \in I$.
40: The **product** is
41: 
42: $$\prod_{i \in I} X_i \;:=\; \Big\{\, x : x \text{ is a function with domain } I \text{ and } x(i) \in X_i \text{ for every } i \in I \,\Big\},$$
43: 
44: and we write $x_i := x(i)$, the $i$-th **coordinate** of $x$. Two elements of the
45: product are equal exactly when they agree at every index, functions being equal
46: when they have the same domain and the same values. For $j \in I$ the $j$-th
47: **projection** is
48: 
49: $$\pi_j : \prod_{i \in I} X_i \to X_j, \qquad \pi_j(x) := x_j .$$
50: 
51: **Notation for a finite product.** For $I = n$ a natural number, which is the set
52: $\{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ of its predecessors, an element of $\prod_{k<n} X_k$ is a
53: function on $n$ and we write it $(x_0, \dots, x_{n-1})$. In particular $I = 2$
54: gives the **binary product**, written $X \times Y$ for $\prod_{i<2} X_i$ with
55: $X_0 = X$ and $X_1 = Y$, whose elements are written $(u,v)$ for the function
56: $0 \mapsto u$, $1 \mapsto v$. This is the only meaning the symbol $X \times Y$
57: carries on this page.
58: 
59: **Two facts about when the product is nonempty, stated because they are used and
60: because they cost something.** If some $X_{i_0}$ is empty then the product is
61: empty, since no function can take a value in $X_{i_0}$. Conversely, suppose every
62: $X_i$ is nonempty.
63: 
64: - For $I = n$ a natural number, the product is nonempty, and this is a theorem of
65:   ZF: [[lem-finite-choice]] applied to the function $i \mapsto X_i$ on $n$
66:   supplies a choice function $g$ for the family of values, and
67:   $x(i) := g(X_i)$ defines a member of $\prod_{k<n} X_k$.
68: - For an arbitrary $I$ the assertion "$\prod_{i \in I} X_i \ne \varnothing$
69:   whenever every $X_i$ is nonempty" **is** the Axiom of Choice: it is the
70:   formulation recorded in [[def-axiom-of-choice]], and the choice function of
71:   [[def-choice-function]] is exactly a point of the product of a family by
72:   itself. Every use of it below is flagged at the step that spends it.
73: 
74: **The box topology.** Now let each $X_i$ carry a topology $\mathcal{T}_i$
75: ([[def-topological-space]]). Put
76: 
77: $$\mathcal{R} \;:=\; \Big\{\, \prod_{i \in I} U_i \;:\; U_i \in \mathcal{T}_i \text{ for every } i \in I \,\Big\},$$
78: 
79: the family of **boxes**. $\mathcal{R}$ is a basis for a topology
80: ([[thm-basis-criterion]]): it contains $\prod_i X_i$, so it covers the product,
81: and it is closed under binary intersections, since
82: 
83: $$\Big(\prod_i U_i\Big) \cap \Big(\prod_i V_i\Big) = \prod_i (U_i \cap V_i)$$
84: 
85: and each $U_i \cap V_i$ is open by (T3). The topology it generates is the **box
86: topology** $\mathcal{T}^{\square}$, and $\mathcal{R}$ is a basis for it
87: ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
88: 
89: **The product topology.** The **product topology** $\mathcal{T}^{\Pi}$ on
90: $\prod_i X_i$ is the initial topology of the family of projections
91: $(\pi_i)_{i \in I}$ ([[def-initial-and-final-topology]]): the topology generated
92: by the subbasis
93: 
94: $$\mathcal{G} \;:=\; \{\, \pi_i^{-1}[U] : i \in I,\ U \in \mathcal{T}_i \,\}, \qquad \pi_i^{-1}[U] = \prod_{j \in I} W_j \ \text{ with } W_i = U \text{ and } W_j = X_j \text{ for } j \ne i .$$
95: 
96: By [[thm-basis-criterion]] the finite intersections of members of $\mathcal{G}$
97: form a basis for $\mathcal{T}^{\Pi}$, and those finite intersections are exactly
98: the boxes with all but finitely many factors unrestricted:
99: 
100: $$\mathcal{R}^{\Pi} \;=\; \Big\{\, \prod_{i \in I} U_i \;:\; U_i \in \mathcal{T}_i \text{ for every } i, \text{ and } U_i = X_i \text{ for all but finitely many } i \,\Big\}.$$
101: 
102: Indeed the intersection of $\pi_{i_1}^{-1}[U_1], \dots, \pi_{i_n}^{-1}[U_n]$ is
103: the box whose factor at $i$ is the intersection of those $U_m$ with $i_m = i$ and
104: is $X_i$ when no $i_m$ equals $i$; and the intersection of no members is the
105: whole product, the box with every factor $X_i$. Conversely a box with
106: $U_i = X_i$ off a finite set is such an intersection. Members of
107: $\mathcal{R}^{\Pi}$ are called **basic product-open** sets, and members of
108: $\mathcal{R}$ **boxes**. So $\mathcal{R}^{\Pi} \subseteq \mathcal{R}$, with
109: equality when $I$ is a natural number.
110: 
111: **The empty product.** For $I = \varnothing$ there is exactly one function with
112: domain $\varnothing$, the empty function, so $\prod_{i \in \varnothing} X_i$ is a
113: one-point set. A one-point set carries exactly one topology, namely
114: $\{\varnothing, \{\varnothing\}\}$, since a topology must contain the empty set
115: and the whole set and there is nothing else to contain
116: ([[def-topological-space]]); so the box topology and the product topology agree
117: there, and both equal the discrete topology and the indiscrete topology
118: ([[def-standard-topologies]]), which coincide on a one-point set. There are no
119: projections to speak of, and the initial topology of the empty family is indeed
120: the indiscrete one ([[def-initial-and-final-topology]]).
121: 
122: **Convention.** Unless the box topology is named explicitly, $\prod_i X_i$ always
123: carries the product topology in this library. That is not a matter of taste: the
124: product topology is the one with the characteristic property of the next item,
125: and the box topology has no such property.
126: 
FILE items/def-quotient-topology.md
43: ## Definition
44: 
45: **The quotient topology.** Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space
46: ([[def-topological-space]]), let $Y$ be a set and let $q : X \to Y$ be a
47: surjection ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]). The **quotient topology** on
48: $Y$ induced by $q$ is the final topology of the one-element family $(q)$
49: ([[def-initial-and-final-topology]]):
50: 
51: $$\mathcal{T}_q \;:=\; \{\, V \subseteq Y : q^{-1}[V] \in \mathcal{T} \,\} .$$
52: 
53: That this is a topology is discharged in [[def-initial-and-final-topology]],
54: where every final topology is verified to satisfy (T1), (T2) and (T3). Dually,
55: $C \subseteq Y$ is closed in $\mathcal{T}_q$ exactly when $q^{-1}[C]$ is closed
56: in $X$, because $q^{-1}[Y \setminus V] = X \setminus q^{-1}[V]$.
57: 
58: **Quotient map.** A surjection $q : X \to Y$ between topological *spaces* is a
59: **quotient map** (also *identification map*) when the topology of $Y$ is the
60: quotient topology of $q$, that is when
61: 
62: $$V \text{ is open in } Y \iff q^{-1}[V] \text{ is open in } X \qquad (V \subseteq Y).$$
63: 
64: The implication from left to right is exactly continuity of $q$
65: ([[def-continuous-map-top]], [[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]] clause
66: (b)), so a quotient map is a continuous surjection with the extra property that
67: its topology is as *fine* as continuity permits. Equivalently, and this form is
68: used as often: $C$ is closed in $Y$ if and only if $q^{-1}[C]$ is closed in $X$.
69: 
70: **Saturated sets.** Let $q : X \to Y$ be a surjection. A subset $A \subseteq X$ is
71: **saturated** with respect to $q$ when
72: 
73: $$A \;=\; q^{-1}\big[\,q[A]\,\big] ,$$
74: 
75: equivalently when $A$ is a union of fibres $q^{-1}[\{y\}]$, equivalently when
76: $x \in A$ and $q(x') = q(x)$ imply $x' \in A$. The set $q^{-1}[q[A]]$ is the
77: **saturation** of $A$, and it is the smallest saturated set containing $A$. The
78: map $V \mapsto q^{-1}[V]$ is a bijection from $\mathcal{P}(Y)$ onto the saturated
79: subsets of $X$, with inverse $A \mapsto q[A]$, since $q$ is surjective; under it
80: the open sets of the quotient topology correspond exactly to the **saturated open
81: subsets of $X$**. That correspondence is the working description of the quotient
82: topology: to know the open sets of $Y$ is to know which open subsets of $X$ are
83: saturated.
84: 
85: **Quotient by an equivalence relation.** Let $\sim$ be an **equivalence relation**
86: on $X$, that is a relation that is reflexive ($x \sim x$), symmetric
87: ($x \sim x'$ implies $x' \sim x$) and transitive ($x \sim x'$ and $x' \sim x''$
88: imply $x \sim x''$). Write $[x] := \{\, x' \in X : x' \sim x \,\}$ for the
89: **equivalence class** of $x$; distinct classes are disjoint and their union is
90: $X$. The **quotient set** is $X/\!\sim\ := \{\, [x] : x \in X \,\}$ and the
91: **canonical projection** is
92: 
93: $$\pi : X \to X/\!\sim, \qquad \pi(x) := [x] ,$$
94: 
95: which is a surjection by construction. The set $X/\!\sim$ always carries the
96: quotient topology of $\pi$, and $(X/\!\sim,\ \mathcal{T}_\pi)$ is called an
97: **identification space**. A subset of $X$ is saturated for $\pi$ exactly when it
98: is a union of equivalence classes.
99: 
100: **A convenient special case: collapsing a subset.** For $\varnothing \ne B \subseteq X$
101: let $\sim_B$ be the relation whose classes are $B$ itself and the singletons
102: $\{x\}$ for $x \notin B$; this is an equivalence relation, its classes being a
103: partition of $X$. The resulting quotient is written $X/B$, and its canonical
104: projection is a surjection identifying all of $B$ to a single point and doing
105: nothing else. Saturated sets for $\sim_B$ are the sets $A$ with $A \cap B \in
106: \{\varnothing, B\}$.
107: 
108: **Two conventions used throughout.** First, "quotient map" is a property of a map
109: *together with the two topologies*, never of the map alone. Second, a quotient
110: topology is determined by $q$ together with the topology on its domain, and not by
111: the underlying pair of sets $(X,Y)$: two different surjections onto the same set
112: can give different topologies, the same surjection gives different topologies when
113: its domain is retopologised, and where more than one is in play the map is named.
114: 
FILE items/def-real-order.md
26: ## Definition
27: 
28: A real $x$ is **positive** when it has a representative $(a_n)$ together
29: with a rational $\delta > 0$ and an index $N$ such that $a_n > \delta$
30: for all $n \ge N$. Define
31: 
32: $$x \le y \iff x = y \;\text{ or }\; y - x \text{ is positive},$$
33: 
34: and $|x| = x$ if $x \ge 0$, $|x| = -x$ otherwise.
35: 
FILE items/def-standard-topologies.md
43: ## Definition
44: 
45: Throughout, a topology is as in [[def-topological-space]], and *finite*,
46: *at most countable* and *uncountable* are as in [[def-countable]], so that
47: "countable" always means "at most countable" and every finite set is countable.
48: Let $X$ be a set. The six families below are topologies on $X$; that each really
49: satisfies (T1), (T2) and (T3) is discharged in full after the list.
50: 
51: 1. **Discrete topology.** $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{disc}} := \mathcal{P}(X)$: every
52:    subset is open, hence every subset is closed, hence every subset is clopen.
53: 2. **Indiscrete topology.** $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{ind}} := \{\varnothing, X\}$.
54:    Its closed sets are again $\varnothing$ and $X$.
55: 3. **Cofinite topology.**
56:    $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cof}} := \{\varnothing\} \cup \{\, U \subseteq X : X \setminus U \text{ is finite} \,\}$.
57:    Its closed sets are $X$ together with the finite subsets of $X$.
58: 4. **Cocountable topology.**
59:    $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}} := \{\varnothing\} \cup \{\, U \subseteq X : X \setminus U \text{ is at most countable} \,\}$.
60:    Its closed sets are $X$ together with the at most countable subsets of $X$.
61: 5. **Particular-point topology.** Fix $p \in X$ and put
62:    $\mathcal{T}_p := \{\varnothing\} \cup \{\, U \subseteq X : p \in U \,\}$: the
63:    open sets are $\varnothing$ and the sets containing $p$. Its closed sets are
64:    $X$ together with the sets **not** containing $p$.
65: 6. **Sierpinski topology.** On a two-point set $S = \{a, b\}$ with $a \ne b$,
66:    $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}} := \{\varnothing, \{b\}, S\}$. The pair
67:    $(S, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}})$ is **Sierpinski space**; $b$ is its open
68:    point and $a$ its closed point. This is exactly the particular-point topology
69:    of item 5 on a two-point set with particular point $b$, listed separately
70:    because it is quoted so often.
71: 
72: **Two elementary facts about finite sets are used below, and both are proved
73: here.**
74: 
75: *(i) A subset of a finite set is finite.* Let $F \approx n$ with $n \in \mathbb{N}$
76: ([[def-equinumerous]], [[def-natural-numbers]]), witnessed by a bijection
77: $\varphi : F \to n$, and let $B \subseteq F$. Then $\varphi$ restricts to a
78: bijection of $B$ onto $\varphi[B] \subseteq n$
79: ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]). Every element of the von Neumann natural
80: $n$ is a natural number strictly smaller than $n$
81: ([[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]), so $\varphi[B]$ is a subset of $\mathbb{N}$
82: bounded above by $n$, hence finite by the sharper form of
83: [[lem-subset-of-countable]] ("a subset $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is finite if it is
84: bounded above"). Since $\approx$ is symmetric and transitive, $B$ is finite.
85: 
86: *(ii) A union of two finite sets is finite.* First, if $H$ is finite and $g$ is
87: any object then $H \cup \{g\}$ is finite: if $g \in H$ there is nothing to prove,
88: and otherwise a bijection $u : H \to k$ extends to a bijection
89: $H \cup \{g\} \to k \cup \{k\} = \sigma(k)$ by setting $u(g) := k$, which is
90: injective because $k \notin k$ ([[lem-nat-transitive-irreflexive]]). Now fix a
91: finite set $F$ and argue by induction ([[thm-induction-principle]]) on
92: $m \in \mathbb{N}$ over the statement "for every $G$ with $G \approx m$, the union
93: $F \cup G$ is finite". At $m = 0$ we have $G = \varnothing$ and $F \cup G = F$. At
94: $m = \sigma(j)$, a bijection $\psi : G \to \sigma(j)$ gives $g := \psi^{-1}(j)$
95: and $G' := G \setminus \{g\} \approx j$ (restrict $\psi$), so
96: $F \cup G = (F \cup G') \cup \{g\}$ is finite by the induction hypothesis and the
97: previous sentence.
98: 
99: **Discharge of the topology axioms.**
100: 
101: *Discrete.* Every subset of $X$ lies in $\mathcal{P}(X)$, so (T1), (T2) and (T3)
102: hold with nothing to check.
103: 
104: *Indiscrete.* (T1) is the definition. For (T2), a subfamily of
105: $\{\varnothing, X\}$ has union $\varnothing$ (if it is empty or $\{\varnothing\}$)
106: or $X$ (otherwise). For (T3), $\varnothing \cap A = \varnothing$ and
107: $X \cap X = X$.
108: 
109: *Cofinite.* (T1): $\varnothing$ is listed, and $X \setminus X = \varnothing$ is
110: finite. (T2): let $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cof}}$. If every
111: member is $\varnothing$ the union is $\varnothing$. Otherwise fix
112: $U_0 \in \mathcal{S}$ with $U_0 \ne \varnothing$; then
113: $X \setminus \bigcup \mathcal{S} \subseteq X \setminus U_0$, which is finite, so
114: the left side is finite by (i). (T3): for nonempty $U, V$ with finite
115: complements, $X \setminus (U \cap V) = (X \setminus U) \cup (X \setminus V)$ is
116: finite by (ii); and if either of $U, V$ is empty so is $U \cap V$. The closed sets
117: are the complements of the open ones, that is $X = X \setminus \varnothing$
118: together with the finite sets.
119: 
120: *Cocountable.* Identical to the cofinite case with "at most countable" in place of
121: "finite": (i) is replaced by [[lem-subset-of-countable]] itself, and (ii) by the
122: statement that a union of two at most countable sets is at most countable, which
123: is the two-set instance of [[thm-countable-union-of-countable]] applied to the
124: family $A_0 := U, A_1 := V, A_k := \varnothing$ for $k \ge 2$.
125: 
126: *Particular point.* (T1): $\varnothing$ is listed and $p \in X$. (T2): a
127: subfamily whose members are all $\varnothing$ has union $\varnothing$; otherwise
128: some member contains $p$, hence so does the union. (T3): if $U$ and $V$ both
129: contain $p$ then so does $U \cap V$; and if either is $\varnothing$ then so is the
130: intersection.
131: 
132: *Sierpinski.* The special case $X = \{a,b\}$, $p = b$ of the previous paragraph:
133: the sets containing $b$ are $\{b\}$ and $S$, so
134: $\mathcal{T}_b = \{\varnothing, \{b\}, S\} = \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}}$.
135: 
FILE items/def-subspace-topology-top.md
32: ## Definition
33: 
34: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and let
35: $S \subseteq X$. The **subspace topology** (also *relative topology*) on $S$ is
36: 
37: $$\mathcal{T}_S := \{\, U \cap S : U \in \mathcal{T} \,\},$$
38: 
39: the family of **traces** on $S$ of the open sets of $X$. The pair
40: $(S, \mathcal{T}_S)$ is a **subspace** of $X$. A subset of $S$ that lies in
41: $\mathcal{T}_S$ is said to be **open in $S$**, and *relatively open* where the
42: ambient space needs emphasis.
43: 
44: **$\mathcal{T}_S$ is a topology, and this is discharged here.** (T1):
45: $\varnothing = \varnothing \cap S$ and $S = X \cap S$ are traces. (T2): if
46: $\mathcal{S}' \subseteq \mathcal{T}_S$, choose for each member a set of
47: $\mathcal{T}$ tracing to it — no choice principle is needed, since
48: $U' := \bigcup \{\, U \in \mathcal{T} : U \cap S \subseteq W \,\}$ is a canonical
49: such set for $W \in \mathcal{T}_S$, being open by (T2) in $X$ and satisfying
50: $U' \cap S = W$ — and then
51: $\bigcup_i (U_i \cap S) = (\bigcup_i U_i) \cap S \in \mathcal{T}_S$ by (T2) in
52: $X$. (T3): $(U \cap S) \cap (V \cap S) = (U \cap V) \cap S \in \mathcal{T}_S$ by
53: (T3) in $X$.
54: 
55: **Closed sets of a subspace are the traces of the closed sets.** A set
56: $C \subseteq S$ is closed in $S$ if and only if $C = F \cap S$ for some closed
57: $F \subseteq X$. Indeed $S \setminus (U \cap S) = (X \setminus U) \cap S$ and
58: $S \setminus (F \cap S) = (X \setminus F) \cap S$, so complementation inside $S$
59: matches complementation inside $X$ under tracing.
60: 
61: **Bases and subbases trace as well.** If $\mathcal{B}$ is a basis for
62: $\mathcal{T}$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]) then
63: $\mathcal{B}_S := \{\, B \cap S : B \in \mathcal{B} \,\}$ is a basis for
64: $\mathcal{T}_S$: its members are open in $S$, and for $W = U \cap S$ open in $S$
65: and $x \in W$ there is $B \in \mathcal{B}$ with $x \in B \subseteq U$, whence
66: $x \in B \cap S \subseteq W$. The same computation with a subbasis $\mathcal{S}$
67: shows that $\{\, S_0 \cap S : S_0 \in \mathcal{S} \,\}$ is a subbasis for
68: $\mathcal{T}_S$, since tracing commutes with finite intersections and with
69: unions.
70: 
71: **The inclusion is continuous.** The inclusion map $\iota : S \to X$,
72: $\iota(s) = s$, satisfies $\iota^{-1}[U] = U \cap S$ for every $U \subseteq X$,
73: so preimages of open sets are open and $\iota$ is continuous
74: ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]], clause (b)). Moreover
75: $\mathcal{T}_S$ is the **coarsest** topology on $S$ making $\iota$ continuous: any
76: topology on $S$ for which $\iota$ is continuous must contain every
77: $\iota^{-1}[U] = U \cap S$, hence contain $\mathcal{T}_S$.
78: 
79: **Characteristic property of a map into a subspace.** Let $(Z, \mathcal{T}_Z)$ be
80: a topological space and let $g : Z \to S$ be a function. Then
81: 
82: $$g \text{ is continuous as a map } Z \to (S,\mathcal{T}_S) \iff \iota \circ g \text{ is continuous as a map } Z \to (X,\mathcal{T}) .$$
83: 
84: *Proof.* For $U \in \mathcal{T}$ one has
85: $(\iota \circ g)^{-1}[U] = g^{-1}[\iota^{-1}[U]] = g^{-1}[U \cap S]$. If $g$ is
86: continuous then each $g^{-1}[U \cap S]$ is open, so $\iota \circ g$ is continuous;
87: conversely if $\iota \circ g$ is continuous then for any $W = U \cap S$ open in
88: $S$ the set $g^{-1}[W] = (\iota \circ g)^{-1}[U]$ is open, so $g$ is continuous.
89: Both directions use only clause (b) of [[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]].
90: 
91: **Restriction of a continuous map.** If $f : X \to Y$ is continuous and
92: $S \subseteq X$, then $f|_S : S \to Y$ is continuous, since
93: $(f|_S)^{-1}[V] = f^{-1}[V] \cap S$ is open in $S$ for every open $V \subseteq Y$
94: ([[def-continuous-map-top]], [[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]]).
95: 
96: **When relative and ambient agree.** If $S$ is open in $X$ then a subset of $S$
97: is open in $S$ if and only if it is open in $X$: a trace $U \cap S$ is then an
98: intersection of two open sets of $X$, and conversely an open subset of $X$
99: contained in $S$ is its own trace. The same statement with "closed" throughout
100: holds when $S$ is closed in $X$. Both are used in the pasting lemma of the next
101: item, and both fail without the hypothesis: $S$ itself is always open and closed
102: in $S$, and need be neither in $X$.
103: 
FILE items/def-topological-space.md
34: ## Definition
35: 
36: Let $X$ be a set and write $\mathcal{P}(X)$ for its power set. A **topology** on
37: $X$ is a family $\mathcal{T} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$ such that
38: 
39: - **(T1)** $\varnothing \in \mathcal{T}$ and $X \in \mathcal{T}$;
40: - **(T2)** $\bigcup \mathcal{S} \in \mathcal{T}$ for every $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$;
41: - **(T3)** $U \cap V \in \mathcal{T}$ for all $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$.
42: 
43: A **topological space** is a pair $(X, \mathcal{T})$ consisting of a set $X$ and
44: a topology $\mathcal{T}$ on it; the elements of $X$ are its **points**. When only
45: one topology is in play we write $X$ for the pair; when several are, the topology
46: is always named.
47: 
48: The members of $\mathcal{T}$ are the **open** sets of $(X,\mathcal{T})$. A subset
49: $F \subseteq X$ is **closed** when its complement $X \setminus F$ is open, and
50: **clopen** when it is both open and closed. By (T1) both $\varnothing$ and $X$
51: are clopen. *Open and closed are not opposites*: a set may be neither, and it may
52: be both, so "not open" is never a synonym for "closed".
53: 
54: **Finite intersections, and the empty one.** Axiom (T3) is stated for two sets
55: because that is all that is ever needed: if $n \ge 1$ and
56: $U_1, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{T}$, then $U_1 \cap \dots \cap U_n \in \mathcal{T}$
57: follows by iterating (T3) $n-1$ times. The intersection of the **empty** family
58: of subsets of $X$ is $X$, since the condition "lies in every member of the empty
59: family" is vacuous; that case is covered by (T1) rather than by (T3), which is
60: one of the two reasons $X \in \mathcal{T}$ is demanded outright. The other is
61: that (T2) alone gives only $\bigcup \varnothing = \varnothing$.
62: 
63: **The closed sets, and the dual axiomatisation.** Put
64: $\mathcal{C} := \{\, X \setminus U : U \in \mathcal{T} \,\}$, the family of closed
65: sets. Then
66: 
67: - **(C1)** $\varnothing \in \mathcal{C}$ and $X \in \mathcal{C}$;
68: - **(C2)** $\bigcap \mathcal{D} \in \mathcal{C}$ for every **nonempty**
69:   $\mathcal{D} \subseteq \mathcal{C}$;
70: - **(C3)** $C \cup D \in \mathcal{C}$ for all $C, D \in \mathcal{C}$.
71: 
72: Conversely, if a family $\mathcal{C} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$ satisfies (C1),
73: (C2) and (C3), then $\mathcal{T} := \{\, X \setminus C : C \in \mathcal{C} \,\}$
74: is a topology on $X$ whose closed sets are exactly the members of $\mathcal{C}$.
75: So a topology may be presented equally well by its open sets or by its closed
76: sets, and this library uses whichever is shorter.
77: 
78: *Both directions are one computation, and it is carried out here rather than
79: assumed.* The map $U \mapsto X \setminus U$ is an involution of
80: $\mathcal{P}(X)$, hence a bijection, and it carries the two De Morgan identities
81: 
82: $$X \setminus \bigcup_{i \in I} A_i = \bigcap_{i \in I} (X \setminus A_i) \quad (I \ne \varnothing), \qquad X \setminus \bigcap_{i \in I} A_i = \bigcup_{i \in I} (X \setminus A_i) \quad (I \ne \varnothing)$$
83: 
84: term by term into each other. Given (T1)–(T3): $\varnothing = X \setminus X$ and
85: $X = X \setminus \varnothing$ lie in $\mathcal{C}$, which is (C1); for nonempty
86: $\mathcal{D} \subseteq \mathcal{C}$ the family
87: $\mathcal{S} = \{\, X \setminus C : C \in \mathcal{D} \,\}$ lies in $\mathcal{T}$
88: and $\bigcap \mathcal{D} = X \setminus \bigcup \mathcal{S}$ is closed by (T2),
89: which is (C2); and $C \cup D = X \setminus ((X \setminus C) \cap (X \setminus D))$
90: is closed by (T3), which is (C3). Given (C1)–(C3) the same three lines run
91: backwards, the empty-union case $\bigcup \varnothing = \varnothing$ being supplied
92: by (C1) rather than by (C2); and $\{\, X \setminus C : C \in \mathcal{C} \,\}$ has
93: $\mathcal{C}$ as its family of complements because complementation is an
94: involution.
95: 
96: **Comparison of topologies.** If $\mathcal{T}_1$ and $\mathcal{T}_2$ are
97: topologies on the same set $X$ and $\mathcal{T}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{T}_2$, then
98: $\mathcal{T}_1$ is **coarser** than $\mathcal{T}_2$ and $\mathcal{T}_2$ is
99: **finer** than $\mathcal{T}_1$; the comparison is **strict** when the inclusion
100: is. Inclusion is a partial order on the set of topologies on $X$
101: ([[def-partial-order]]), and it is not a total one: two topologies on the same
102: set may be incomparable.
103: 
FILE items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md
34: ## Definition
35: 
36: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]).
37: 
38: A family $\mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ is a **basis for $\mathcal{T}$** if
39: every open set is a union of members of $\mathcal{B}$: for every
40: $U \in \mathcal{T}$ there is $\mathcal{B}_U \subseteq \mathcal{B}$ with
41: $U = \bigcup \mathcal{B}_U$. Equivalently, and this is the form used in proofs,
42: 
43: $$\text{for every } U \in \mathcal{T} \text{ and every } x \in U \text{ there is } B \in \mathcal{B} \text{ with } x \in B \subseteq U .$$
44: 
45: *The two forms say the same thing.* If every open $U$ is such a union and
46: $x \in U$, then $x$ lies in one of the sets united, which is a member of
47: $\mathcal{B}$ inside $U$. Conversely, if the displayed condition holds then
48: $U = \bigcup \{\, B \in \mathcal{B} : B \subseteq U \,\}$, since each such $B$ is
49: contained in $U$ and each $x \in U$ lies in one of them. Note that
50: $\bigcup \varnothing = \varnothing$, so the empty open set is covered by the empty
51: subfamily and needs no member of $\mathcal{B}$. The members of a basis are called
52: **basic open sets**.
53: 
54: **The topology generated by a family.** Let $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$
55: be *any* family of subsets of $X$. Then
56: 
57: $$\langle \mathcal{S} \rangle := \bigcap \{\, \mathcal{T}' : \mathcal{T}' \text{ is a topology on } X \text{ with } \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T}' \,\}$$
58: 
59: is a topology on $X$, it contains $\mathcal{S}$, and it is contained in every
60: topology on $X$ that contains $\mathcal{S}$. It is called the **topology
61: generated by $\mathcal{S}$**, and $\mathcal{S}$ is a **subbasis** for a topology
62: $\mathcal{T}$ when $\mathcal{T} = \langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$.
63: 
64: *This is well posed, and the obligation is discharged here.* The collection being
65: intersected is nonempty, because $\mathcal{P}(X)$ is a topology on $X$ containing
66: $\mathcal{S}$; so the intersection is an intersection of a nonempty family of
67: subsets of $\mathcal{P}(X)$ and is a set. It is a topology: $\varnothing$ and $X$
68: lie in every topology on $X$, hence in the intersection, which is (T1); if
69: $\mathcal{S}' \subseteq \langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$ then $\mathcal{S}'$ is a
70: subfamily of each $\mathcal{T}'$ in the collection, so $\bigcup \mathcal{S}'$ lies
71: in each $\mathcal{T}'$ and hence in the intersection, which is (T2); and the same
72: argument with $U \cap V$ gives (T3). It contains $\mathcal{S}$ because every
73: $\mathcal{T}'$ in the collection does, and it is contained in each such
74: $\mathcal{T}'$ because an intersection is contained in each of its members. So
75: $\langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$ is the coarsest topology on $X$ containing
76: $\mathcal{S}$ ([[def-topological-space]]), and in particular it is uniquely
77: determined by $\mathcal{S}$.
78: 
79: **Neither notion is intrinsic to the family alone.** "$\mathcal{B}$ is a basis
80: for $\mathcal{T}$" and "$\mathcal{S}$ is a subbasis for $\mathcal{T}$" are
81: relations between a family and a topology, not properties of the family. The
82: question of which families are a basis *for some* topology, and how the topology
83: generated by a subbasis is computed from it, is settled by the next item.
84: 
FILE items/def-upper-bound.md
33: ## Definition
34: 
35: Let $(P, \le)$ be a poset ([[def-partial-order]]) and $S \subseteq P$.
36: 
37: An element $u \in P$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \le u$ for every
38: $s \in S$.
39: 
40: An element $u \in P$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**) of $S$ if $u$
41: is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \le v$ for every upper bound $v$ of $S$. When it
42: exists we write $u = \sup S$.
43: 
44: An element $u \in P$ is a **strict upper bound** of $S$ if $s < u$ for every
45: $s \in S$.
46: 

