FILE items/cor-archimedean-reciprocal.md
35: ## Statement
36: 
37: Let $F$ be a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and let
38: $\varepsilon \in F$ with $\varepsilon > 0$. Then there is a natural number
39: $n \ge 1$ such that
40: 
41: $$\frac{1}{n \cdot 1_F} < \varepsilon ,$$
42: 
43: where $n \cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of $F$ ([[thm-of-archimedean]]) and
44: $1/(n \cdot 1_F)$ is its multiplicative inverse ([[def-field]]). As is standard
45: we abbreviate $n \cdot 1_F$ to $n$ and write the conclusion $1/n < \varepsilon$.
46: 
47: This is the **reciprocal form** of the Archimedean property.
48: [[thm-of-archimedean]] on its own delivers only the assertion that the canonical
49: naturals are cofinal, $x < n \cdot 1_F$; the form actually used in analysis, that
50: the reciprocals of the naturals get below every positive bound, is the statement
51: above, and it is recorded separately so that no proof has to reconstruct the
52: inversion step in passing.
53: 
FILE items/cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology.md
38: ## Statement
39: 
40: Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $n \ge 1$, let $\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions
41: $n \to \mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]) carrying the product topology of $n$
42: copies of the usual topology of $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-product-topology]]), and let
43: $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric. Then:
44: 
45: 1. The product topology on $\mathbb{R}^n$ **is** the metric topology of $d_2$
46:    ([[def-metric-topology]]), so $\mathbb{R}^n$ as a product and $\mathbb{R}^n$ as
47:    a metric space are one topological space, and it is metrizable
48:    ([[def-metrizable-space]]).
49: 2. A subset $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset for the product
50:    topology ([[def-compact-space]]) **if and only if** $K$ is closed in
51:    $\mathbb{R}^n$ and bounded ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).
52: 
53: The hypothesis $n \ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines
54: $\mathbb{R}^n$ and its three metrics only there; for $n = 0$ the product is a
55: one-point space and is compact. **No choice principle is used**: the metric
56: statement it is read off from is proved by bisection
57: ([[thm-heine-borel-rn]]).
58: 
FILE items/cor-metrizability-and-first-countability-are-hereditary.md
38: ## Statement
39: 
40: Both of the following properties of topological spaces are **hereditary**
41: ([[def-hereditary-property]]).
42: 
43: 1. **Metrizability** ([[def-metrizable-space]]). If $\mathcal{T}$ is induced by a
44:    metric $d$ on $X$ and $S \subseteq X$, then the subspace topology
45:    $\mathcal{T}_S$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]) is induced by the subspace
46:    metric $d_S = d \restriction (S \times S)$
47:    ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]). So a subspace of a metrizable space
48:    is metrizable, and a metric inducing its topology is available explicitly and
49:    not merely asserted to exist.
50: 2. **First countability** ([[def-first-countable-top]]). If every point of $X$
51:    has an at most countable neighbourhood base and $S \subseteq X$, then every
52:    point of $S$ has an at most countable neighbourhood base in
53:    $(S, \mathcal{T}_S)$, namely the family of traces on $S$ of the members of a
54:    base at that point in $X$.
55: 
56: Claim 1 is a corollary in the strict sense: the identification of the subspace
57: topology with the metric topology of the subspace metric is discharged inside
58: [[def-metrizable-space]], and nothing is reproved here.
59: 
FILE items/lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient.md
31: ## Statement
32: 
33: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), let
34: $A \subseteq X$ and let $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ be the subspace
35: ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]). Then:
36: 
37: 1. **Compactness read in the ambient space.** $A$ is a compact subset of $X$
38:    ([[def-compact-space]]), that is $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is a compact space, if
39:    and only if for every family $\mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ with
40:    $A \subseteq \bigcup \mathcal{U}$ there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
41:    $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with $A \subseteq U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n$,
42:    or else $A = \varnothing$.
43: 2. **The same in indexed form.** $A$ is a compact subset of $X$ if and only if
44:    for every set $I$ and every family $(U_i)_{i \in I}$ of open subsets of $X$
45:    with $A \subseteq \bigcup_{i \in I} U_i$ there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
46:    indices $i_0, \dots, i_n \in I$ with
47:    $A \subseteq U_{i_0} \cup \dots \cup U_{i_n}$, or else $A = \varnothing$.
48: 
49: Claim 2 is the form used by almost every later proof on this page, because a
50: cover is usually produced by a rule that attaches an open set to each point or to
51: each index, and a set of open sets forgets that rule. No choice principle is used
52: anywhere below; the one place a selection is made is over a finite index set, and
53: [[lem-finite-choice]] is a theorem of ZF.
54: 
FILE items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md
42: ## Statement
43: 
44: Let $A$ be a nonempty set. Then $A$ is at most countable ([[def-countable]]) if
45: and only if there is a surjection $s : \mathbb{N} \to A$
46: ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
47: 
48: Moreover, from any such surjection an injection $A \to \mathbb{N}$ is obtained
49: explicitly, without any choice, by
50: 
51: $$j(a) := \min\{\, k \in \mathbb{N} : s(k) = a \,\}.$$
52: 
53: This is the working form of countability used everywhere below: to prove a
54: nonempty set countable it suffices to list its elements, repetitions and all.
55: 
56: **No choice principle is used.** The backward direction is where an appeal to
57: choice would be natural ("for each $a$ pick some $k$ with $s(k) = a$") and it is
58: avoided outright, because $\min$ is canonical: every nonempty set of naturals has
59: a *least* element ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]), so $j(a)$ is determined by
60: $a$ and $s$ alone.
61: 
FILE items/lem-metrics-on-rn.md
44: ## Statement
45: 
46: Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $n \ge 1$. A von Neumann natural is the set of its
47: predecessors, $n = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), so it can be
48: used directly as an index set. Define
49: 
50: $$\mathbb{R}^n := \{\, x : x \text{ is a function } n \to \mathbb{R} \,\},$$
51: 
52: and write $x_k$ for $x(k)$, $k < n$. Two elements of $\mathbb{R}^n$ are equal
53: exactly when they agree at every $k < n$, functions being equal when they have
54: the same values. For $x, y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ put
55: 
56: $$d_1(x,y) := \sum_{k<n} |x_k - y_k|, \qquad d_2(x,y) := \sqrt{\ \sum_{k<n} (x_k - y_k)^2\ }, \qquad d_\infty(x,y) := \max\{\, |x_k - y_k| : k < n \,\}.$$
57: 
58: All three are well defined: the finite sums are those of [[def-finite-sum]]; the
59: sum of squares is nonnegative ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]], [[lem-of-square-positive]])
60: so it has a unique nonnegative square root ([[thm-of-square-roots]]); and
61: $\{|x_k - y_k| : k < n\}$ is a nonempty finite subset of $\mathbb{R}$, because
62: $n \ge 1$, so it has a maximum ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]], [[def-max-min]]).
63: 
64: **Then $d_1$, $d_2$ and $d_\infty$ are metrics on $\mathbb{R}^n$**
65: ([[def-metric-space]]).
66: 
67: **Why $n \ge 1$.** For $n = 0$ the set $\mathbb{R}^0$ has exactly one element,
68: the empty function, and $d_1$ and $d_2$ are the empty sum $0$ and its root; but
69: $d_\infty$ would be the maximum of the empty set, which does not exist. The
70: hypothesis $n \ge 1$ is therefore not decoration, and it is carried by every
71: statement about $d_\infty$ in this library.
72: 
FILE items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md
34: ## Statement
35: 
36: Let $F$ be an ordered field, with field structure as in [[def-field]], and for
37: $n \ge 1$ let $n \cdot 1_F$ denote the canonical natural number
38: $\underbrace{1_F + \cdots + 1_F}_{n}$, defined recursively by $1 \cdot 1_F = 1_F$
39: and $(n+1) \cdot 1_F = n \cdot 1_F + 1_F$. Then for every $n \ge 1$:
40: 
41: - $n \cdot 1_F > 0$ and $(n+1) \cdot 1_F > n \cdot 1_F$;
42: - the map $n \mapsto n \cdot 1_F$ is strictly increasing, hence injective, on $\{1, 2, 3, \dots\}$;
43: - $(m+n) \cdot 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + n \cdot 1_F$ and $(mn) \cdot 1_F = (m \cdot 1_F)(n \cdot 1_F)$ for all $m, n \ge 1$.
44: 
FILE items/lem-of-q-dense.md
30: ## Statement
31: 
32: Let $F$ be an Archimedean ordered field ([[def-archimedean-field]]) and let
33: $\iota : \mathbb{Q} \to F$ be the canonical embedding ([[lem-of-q-embeds]]). Then
34: $\iota(\mathbb{Q})$ is dense in $F$: for any $x < y$ in $F$ there is a rational
35: $q$ with $x < \iota(q) < y$.
36: 
FILE items/lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal.md
42: ## Statement
43: 
44: Let $\mathbb{N} = \omega$ be the natural numbers ([[def-natural-numbers]]) with
45: their usual order ([[def-nat-order]]). Then:
46: 
47: **(i)** for all $m, n \in \mathbb{N}$, $m \in n$ if and only if $m < n$;
48: 
49: **(ii)** every natural number is an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]), and $\omega$ is an
50: ordinal;
51: 
52: **(iii)** $\omega$ is a limit ordinal ([[def-limit-ordinal]]);
53: 
54: **(iv)** every ordinal $\alpha \in \omega$ is $0$ or a successor ordinal, and
55: consequently $\omega$ is the **least** limit ordinal: $\omega \subseteq \gamma$,
56: that is $\omega \le \gamma$, for every limit ordinal $\gamma$.
57: 
58: So the natural numbers are exactly the ordinals below $\omega$, and $\omega$ is
59: the first ordinal at which induction acquires a limit clause.
60: 
61: **Everything here is a theorem of ZF, and no choice principle is used.** The
62: only axiom beyond the basic ones that any of it needs is Infinity, which is what
63: makes $\omega$ a set at all ([[lem-omega-smallest-inductive]]).
64: 
FILE items/lem-ordinal-basics.md
36: ## Statement
37: 
38: Let $\alpha$ and $\beta$ be ordinals ([[def-ordinal]]). Then:
39: 
40: **(a)** every element of $\alpha$ is an ordinal;
41: 
42: **(b)** $\alpha \notin \alpha$;
43: 
44: **(c)** $\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ is an ordinal;
45: 
46: **(d)** if $A$ is a **nonempty** set of ordinals then $\bigcap A$ is an ordinal;
47: 
48: **(e)** if $A$ is any set of ordinals then $\bigcup A$ is an ordinal;
49: 
50: **(f)** $\alpha \subseteq \beta$ if and only if $\alpha \in \beta$ or
51: $\alpha = \beta$;
52: 
53: **(g)** any two ordinals are comparable under inclusion: $\alpha \subseteq \beta$
54: or $\beta \subseteq \alpha$.
55: 
56: Everything here is a theorem of ZF and uses no choice principle.
57: 
FILE items/lem-product-topology-on-rn.md
40: ## Statement
41: 
42: Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $n \ge 1$, and give $\mathbb{R}$ its usual topology,
43: the metric topology of $d_{\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$
44: ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-metrizable-space]]). Let
45: 
46: $$\mathbb{R}^n \;=\; \prod_{k < n} \mathbb{R}$$
47: 
48: be the product of $n$ copies of $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-product-topology]]). As a
49: **set** this is literally the $\mathbb{R}^n$ of [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], both being
50: the set of functions $n \to \mathbb{R}$; and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\infty$ are the
51: three metrics defined there. Then:
52: 
53: 1. The product topology on $\mathbb{R}^n$ is the metric topology of $d_\infty$
54:    ([[def-metric-topology]]). The key computation is that a $d_\infty$-ball is a
55:    box:
56:    $$B_{d_\infty}(x, r) \;=\; \prod_{k<n} (x_k - r,\ x_k + r) \qquad (r > 0),$$
57:    a product of bounded open intervals ([[def-interval]]).
58: 2. $d_\infty \le d_1 \le n\, d_\infty$ and $d_\infty \le d_2 \le n\, d_\infty$
59:    pointwise, so $d_1$ and $d_2$ are each Lipschitz equivalent to $d_\infty$
60:    ([[def-equivalent-metrics]]); here $n$ denotes the canonical natural
61:    $n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}}$.
62: 3. Consequently all three metrics induce the product topology
63:    ([[thm-metric-equivalence-hierarchy]]). So $\mathbb{R}^n$ carrying the product
64:    topology and $\mathbb{R}^n$ carrying the topology of any one of $d_1$, $d_2$,
65:    $d_\infty$ are **one topological space**, and it is metrizable
66:    ([[def-metrizable-space]]).
67: 
68: **Why $n \ge 1$.** The metric $d_\infty$ is a maximum over $n$ terms, which does
69: not exist for $n = 0$; [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] carries the same hypothesis, and it
70: is carried here for the same reason. For $n = 0$ the product is a one-point space
71: and there is nothing to compare.
72: 
FILE items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md
35: ## Statement
36: 
37: Write $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$ for the image of $\mathbb{Q}$ in $\mathbb{R}$
38: under the canonical embedding $q \mapsto \hat q$ ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]), the
39: set usually written $\mathbb{Q}$ once the identification is made, and put
40: $X := \mathbb{R} \setminus \mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$ for the irrationals. Then:
41: 
42: 1. $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$ is dense in $\mathbb{R}$, that is,
43:    $\overline{\mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}} = \mathbb{R}$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]);
44: 2. $X$ is dense in $\mathbb{R}$;
45: 3. every nonempty open subset of $\mathbb{R}$ is uncountable
46:    ([[def-countable]]).
47: 
48: Claim 2 is not a symmetry of claim 1: the rationals are dense because they are
49: *constructed* to approximate, whereas the irrationals are dense because there are
50: too many points in any interval for a countable set to exhaust it, which is why
51: claim 3 is proved alongside and used for it.
52: 
FILE items/lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree.md
35: ## Statement
36: 
37: Let $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ carry the subspace metric of the usual metric
38: $d_{\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$ of $\mathbb{R}$, that is
39: $d_A(x,y) = |x - y|$ for $x, y \in A$
40: ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]),
41: and let $f : A \to \mathbb{R}$, regarded also as a map of metric spaces
42: $(A, d_A) \to (\mathbb{R}, d_{\mathbb{R}})$. Then the $\mathbb{R}$-native
43: notions of this page and the metric-space notions of the earlier pages are the
44: **same notions**, in the following five senses.
45: 
46: 1. **Continuity.** For every $c \in A$: $f$ is continuous at $c$ in the sense of
47:    [[def-continuity-real]] if and only if $f$ is continuous at $c$ in the sense
48:    of [[def-metric-continuity]]. Consequently $f$ is continuous on $A$ if and
49:    only if it is continuous as a map of metric spaces.
50: 2. **Uniform continuity.** $f$ is uniformly continuous on $A$ in the sense of
51:    [[def-uniform-continuity-real]] if and only if it is uniformly continuous as
52:    a map of metric spaces ([[def-metric-uniform-continuity]]).
53: 3. **Lipschitz.** For a real $L \ge 0$: $f$ is Lipschitz with constant $L$ as a
54:    map of metric spaces ([[def-lipschitz-holder-contraction]]) if and only if
55:    $$|f(x) - f(x')| \le L\,|x - x'| \qquad \text{for all } x, x' \in A .$$
56:    This displayed condition is what "$f$ is Lipschitz with constant $L$" means
57:    for a real function on $A$ in this library; no second definition is made.
58: 4. **Hölder.** For a rational $\alpha$ with $0 < \alpha \le 1$ and a real
59:    $C \ge 0$: $f$ is $\alpha$-Hölder with constant $C$ as a map of metric spaces
60:    if and only if
61:    $$|f(x) - f(x')| \le C\,|x - x'|^{\alpha} \qquad \text{for all } x, x' \in A ,$$
62:    the power being the rational power of a nonnegative base
63:    ([[def-rational-power]]).
64: 5. **Compactness, in both senses used in this library.** For $K \subseteq
65:    \mathbb{R}$ with the subspace metric $d_K$:
66:    - $K$ is compact in the open-cover sense of [[def-open-cover-r]] — every
67:      family of open subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ covering $K$ has a finite subfamily
68:      covering $K$ — if and only if the metric space $(K, d_K)$ is compact
69:      ([[def-metric-compactness]]);
70:    - $K$ is sequentially compact in the sense of [[def-open-cover-r]] if and
71:      only if $(K, d_K)$ is sequentially compact as a metric space
72:      ([[def-metric-compactness-variants]]).
73: 
74: Two consequences are recorded, since they are the reason the dictionary is
75: stated as a lemma rather than as a remark.
76: 
77: 6. **The regularity hierarchy transfers verbatim.** By clauses 1 to 4 and
78:    [[thm-metric-regularity-hierarchy]]: a Lipschitz $f : A \to \mathbb{R}$ is
79:    uniformly continuous on $A$; an $\alpha$-Hölder $f$ with rational
80:    $0 < \alpha \le 1$ is uniformly continuous on $A$; a uniformly continuous $f$
81:    is continuous on $A$; and if $A$ is nonempty and bounded, a Lipschitz $f$ is
82:    $\alpha$-Hölder for every rational $\alpha$ with $0 < \alpha \le 1$. No
83:    strictness is claimed here, and none is claimed there.
84: 7. **Cauchy sequences transfer.** A sequence $(x_k)$ with terms in $A$ is Cauchy
85:    in $(A, d_A)$ ([[def-cauchy-in-metric]]) if and only if it is Cauchy as a
86:    sequence of reals ([[def-real-limit]]); so by clause 2 and
87:    [[thm-uniform-continuity-preserves-cauchy]], a uniformly continuous
88:    $f : A \to \mathbb{R}$ carries Cauchy sequences of $A$ to Cauchy sequences of
89:    $\mathbb{R}$.
90: 
91: **Why this lemma exists, and why it is a lemma.** Three results of this page —
92: [[thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-set-r]], [[thm-extreme-value-r]] and
93: [[thm-heine-cantor-r]] — are stated a second time here, having already been
94: proved metric-generally as
95: [[thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]],
96: [[thm-extreme-value-metric]] and [[thm-heine-cantor-metric]]. The duplication is
97: deliberate: the $\mathbb{R}$-native proofs run through
98: [[thm-heine-borel-characterisation-r]] and
99: [[thm-compact-iff-sequentially-compact-r]], which are order-based, while the
100: metric proofs run through the cover machinery of metric spaces. **This item is
101: the single place in the library where that duplication is acknowledged**, and
102: clauses 1 and 5 are what make the two families of statements literally the same
103: statements. It is a lemma, and not a remark, precisely so that later pages can
104: cite it and move between the two vocabularies.
105: 
106: **Clause 5 closes a second seam.** The phrase *compact subset of $\mathbb{R}$*
107: is defined twice in this library — metrically, as compactness of the metric
108: subspace ([[def-metric-compactness]]), and $\mathbb{R}$-natively, by covers by
109: open subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-open-cover-r]]) — and until this clause no
110: item asserted that the two agree.
111: 
FILE items/lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space.md
41: ## Statement
42: 
43: Define $d_{\mathbb{R}} : \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ by
44: $d_{\mathbb{R}}(x,y) := |x - y|$ ([[def-abs-value]]). Then:
45: 
46: 1. $d_{\mathbb{R}}$ is a metric on $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-metric-space]]); it is
47:    called the **usual metric** of $\mathbb{R}$.
48: 2. For $x \in \mathbb{R}$ and $r > 0$ the open ball is the bounded open interval
49:    ([[def-interval]], [[def-metric-ball]])
50:    $$B(x,r) = (x-r,\ x+r),$$
51:    and the closed ball is $\bar B(x,r) = [x-r,\ x+r]$.
52: 3. Consequently $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open in the metric topology of
53:    $d_{\mathbb{R}}$ ([[def-metric-topology]]) exactly when for every $x \in U$
54:    there is $r > 0$ with $(x-r, x+r) \subseteq U$. This topology is called the
55:    **usual topology** of $\mathbb{R}$.
56: 4. $(\mathbb{R}, d_{\mathbb{R}})$ is not a bounded metric space
57:    ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]): no ball contains $\mathbb{R}$, so
58:    $\operatorname{diam}(\mathbb{R})$ is not defined.
59: 
FILE items/lem-subset-of-countable.md
42: ## Statement
43: 
44: Let $A$ be at most countable ([[def-countable]]) and let $B \subseteq A$. Then
45: $B$ is at most countable.
46: 
47: The proof establishes the sharper statement about subsets of $\mathbb{N}$ from
48: which this follows: **a subset $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is finite if it is
49: bounded above, and countably infinite if it is not.**
50: 
51: **No choice principle is used.** This is the point of the lemma rather than a
52: footnote to it. The enumeration of an unbounded $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is built
53: by always taking the *least* element of $S$ above the previous one, and the least
54: element of a nonempty set of naturals is canonical
55: ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]): it is determined by $S$, not selected from it.
56: Replacing "least" by "some" would turn the construction into an appeal to
57: dependent choice.
58: 
FILE items/lem-sup-epsilon.md
43: ## Statement
44: 
45: Let $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded above, and let $u$ be an
46: upper bound of $S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then
47: 
48: $$u = \sup S \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad \text{for every } \varepsilon > 0 \text{ there exists } s \in S \text{ with } u - \varepsilon < s.$$
49: 
50: In words: among the upper bounds of $S$, the supremum is exactly the one that
51: cannot be lowered by any positive amount and still bound $S$.
52: 
FILE items/lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal.md
38: ## Statement
39: 
40: Let $\gamma$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]), regarded as the set of ordinals
41: below it, linearly ordered by membership ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]), and give it
42: the order topology ([[def-order-topology-on-a-linearly-ordered-set]]). For
43: $\alpha, \beta \in \gamma$ write
44: 
45: $$[0,\beta] := \{\, \xi \in \gamma : \xi \le \beta \,\}, \qquad (\alpha,\beta] := \{\, \xi \in \gamma : \alpha < \xi \le \beta \,\} .$$
46: 
47: Then:
48: 
49: 1. Every set of either form is **clopen** ([[def-topological-space]]), and
50:    $$\mathcal{B}_\gamma \;:=\; \{\, [0,\beta] : \beta \in \gamma \,\} \;\cup\; \{\, (\alpha,\beta] : \alpha, \beta \in \gamma \,\}$$
51:    is a basis for the order topology of $\gamma$
52:    ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
53: 2. The isolated points of $\gamma$ ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]) are
54:    exactly the ordinals $\xi \in \gamma$ that are $0$ or a successor; a limit
55:    ordinal $\xi \in \gamma$ ([[def-limit-ordinal]]) is not isolated.
56: 3. $\gamma$ with its order topology is Hausdorff ([[def-hausdorff-space]]).
57: 
58: **Regularity is not claimed here**, and nothing below asserts any separation
59: property beyond claim 3; the finer separation axioms are not available at this
60: point in the reading order.
61: 

