FILE items/thm-alexander-subbase-lemma.md
32: ## Statement
33: 
34: **Assume the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]), in the form of Zorn's
35: lemma ([[thm-zorn]]), the two being equivalent over ZF ([[cor-ac-iff-zorn]]).
36: 
37: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and let
38: $\mathcal{S}$ be a subbasis for $\mathcal{T}$
39: ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]). Suppose that
40: 
41: > every family $\mathcal{S}_0 \subseteq \mathcal{S}$ with
42: > $X = \bigcup \mathcal{S}_0$ has a finite subfamily whose union is $X$.
43: 
44: Then $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is compact ([[def-compact-space]]).
45: 
46: The converse is immediate and is not the content: a compact space has a finite
47: subcover for **every** open cover, subbasic or not. What the lemma says is that
48: the subbasic covers alone already decide compactness, and that is what makes it
49: usable — a product topology is presented by a subbasis, and the subbasic covers
50: of a product are far easier to handle than its arbitrary open covers.
51: 
FILE items/thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions.md
33: ## Statement
34: 
35: Let $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, let $f, g : A \to \mathbb{R}$, let
36: $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}$ and let $c \in A$. Suppose $f$ and $g$ are continuous at
37: $c$ ([[def-continuity-real]]). Then:
38: 
39: 1. $f + g$, $\alpha f$ and $fg$ are continuous at $c$;
40: 2. $|f|$, the function $x \mapsto |f(x)|$, is continuous at $c$;
41: 3. $\max\{f,g\}$ and $\min\{f,g\}$, defined pointwise by
42:    $x \mapsto \max\{f(x), g(x)\}$ and $x \mapsto \min\{f(x), g(x)\}$
43:    ([[def-max-min]]), are continuous at $c$;
44: 4. if $g(c) \ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \{\, x \in A : g(x) \ne 0 \,\}$, the
45:    point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and the quotient
46:    $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \to \mathbb{R}$, $x \mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is continuous at
47:    $c$ as a function on $A_0$.
48: 
49: Moreover, with no hypothesis at all:
50: 
51: 5. every constant function $A \to \mathbb{R}$ and the identity
52:    $\mathrm{id} : A \to \mathbb{R}$, $x \mapsto x$, are continuous on $A$; hence
53:    so is $x \mapsto x^{n}$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$
54:    ([[def-integer-power]]), and hence so is every **polynomial function**
55:    $x \mapsto a_0 + a_1 x + \dots + a_n x^{n}$ with real coefficients.
56: 
57: Consequently, if $f$ and $g$ are continuous **on** $A$ then so are $f+g$,
58: $\alpha f$, $fg$, $|f|$, $\max\{f,g\}$ and $\min\{f,g\}$, and
59: $(f/g)|_{A_0}$ is continuous on $A_0$.
60: 
61: **Claim 4 is stated on $A_0$ because $f/g$ is not defined where $g$ vanishes**,
62: and $g$ may well vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$. The hypothesis is
63: $g(c) \ne 0$, not "$g$ nowhere zero"; what it buys is that $c$ itself lies in
64: the smaller domain, which is what makes continuity there mean anything.
65: 
66: **Nothing here is proved through a sequence.** Claims 1 and 4 are read off from
67: [[thm-algebra-of-function-limits]], which is itself proved from $\varepsilon$
68: and $\delta$, and claims 2, 3 and 5 are proved directly below. So no choice
69: principle is used anywhere in this item.
70: 
FILE items/thm-basis-criterion.md
35: ## Statement
36: 
37: Let $X$ be a set, and for $\mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$ write
38: 
39: $$\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{B}} := \{\, U \subseteq X : \text{for every } x \in U \text{ there is } B \in \mathcal{B} \text{ with } x \in B \subseteq U \,\} .$$
40: 
41: 1. $\mathcal{B}$ is a basis for some topology on $X$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]])
42:    if and only if
43: 
44:    - **(B1)** $\bigcup \mathcal{B} = X$, and
45:    - **(B2)** for all $B_1, B_2 \in \mathcal{B}$ and every $x \in B_1 \cap B_2$
46:      there is $B_3 \in \mathcal{B}$ with $x \in B_3 \subseteq B_1 \cap B_2$.
47: 
48:    When (B1) and (B2) hold, that topology is **unique**: it is
49:    $\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{B}}$, which is also exactly the family of all unions of
50:    subfamilies of $\mathcal{B}$.
51: 
52: 2. Let $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$ be an arbitrary family and let
53:    $\mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{S}}$ be the family of intersections of finitely many
54:    members of $\mathcal{S}$. Then $\mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{S}}$ satisfies (B1) and
55:    (B2), and $\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{S}}} = \langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$,
56:    the topology generated by $\mathcal{S}$. So the finite intersections of any
57:    subbasis form a basis for the topology it generates.
58: 
59: **The nullary intersection: this library takes the empty intersection to be $X$.**
60: In claim 2 the phrase "finitely many" includes **none**, and the intersection of
61: the empty subfamily of $\mathcal{S}$ is $X$, because the defining condition "lies
62: in every member of the empty family" holds of every point of $X$. Hence
63: $X \in \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{S}}$ for every $\mathcal{S}$, including
64: $\mathcal{S} = \varnothing$, and **no covering hypothesis is imposed on a
65: subbasis**. The competing convention takes only nonempty finite intersections and
66: compensates by requiring $\bigcup \mathcal{S} = X$; under it claim 2 holds
67: verbatim once that hypothesis is added, and the two conventions differ only in
68: which of the two devices supplies (B1). The choice made here is recorded again
69: among this page's conventions, and it is the reason
70: $\langle \varnothing \rangle$ comes out as the indiscrete topology
71: $\{\varnothing, X\}$ rather than being undefined.
72: 
FILE items/thm-cantor-set-properties.md
32: ## Statement
33: 
34: Let $C$ be the Cantor set ([[def-cantor-set]]). Then:
35: 
36: 1. $C$ is closed and bounded, hence compact
37:    ([[thm-heine-borel-characterisation-r]], [[def-open-cover-r]]);
38: 2. $C$ has content zero, and therefore measure zero
39:    ([[def-measure-zero-and-content-zero]]);
40: 3. $C$ is perfect ([[def-perfect-set-r]]);
41: 4. $C$ is uncountable ([[def-countable]]);
42: 5. $C$ contains no interval with two distinct endpoints, and is nowhere dense
43:    ([[def-nowhere-dense-meager]]);
44: 6. every nonempty connected subset of $C$ ([[def-connected-r]]) is a single
45:    point.
46: 
47: Claim 6 is what the phrase "totally disconnected" names elsewhere; that phrase
48: is **not** used here, because no definition of total disconnectedness exists at
49: this point in the reading order. What is proved is exactly the displayed
50: statement, and it is obtained from claim 5 through
51: [[thm-connected-subsets-of-r-are-intervals]].
52: 
FILE items/thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact.md
39: ## Statement
40: 
41: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), with
42: subspaces as in [[def-subspace-topology-top]] and compactness as in
43: [[def-compact-space]]. Then:
44: 
45: 1. **Closed in compact is compact.** If $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is compact and
46:    $F \subseteq X$ is closed in $X$, then $F$ is a compact subset of $X$.
47: 2. **Finite unions.** If $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $K_0, \dots, K_n$ are compact
48:    subsets of $X$, then $K_0 \cup \dots \cup K_n$ is a compact subset of $X$. The
49:    union of the empty list is $\varnothing$, which is a compact subset of every
50:    space.
51: 
52: Claim 1 needs $X$ to be compact and claim 2 does not; no hypothesis of any kind
53: is placed on $X$ in claim 2. **No choice principle is used**: claim 1 selects
54: nothing, taking a least index where a selection would be natural, and claim 2
55: makes finitely many selections through [[lem-finite-choice]], a theorem of ZF.
56: 
FILE items/thm-closure-characterisation-top.md
35: ## Statement
36: 
37: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space, let $\mathcal{B}$ be a basis for
38: $\mathcal{T}$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]), let $A \subseteq X$ and let
39: $x \in X$. Closure, derived set and limit points are as in
40: [[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]. Then:
41: 
42: 1. The following four conditions are equivalent.
43:    - (a) $x \in \overline{A}$;
44:    - (b) $N \cap A \ne \varnothing$ for every neighbourhood $N$ of $x$
45:      ([[def-neighbourhood-top]]);
46:    - (c) $U \cap A \ne \varnothing$ for every open $U$ with $x \in U$;
47:    - (d) $B \cap A \ne \varnothing$ for every $B \in \mathcal{B}$ with $x \in B$.
48: 2. $\overline{A}$ is closed, contains $A$, and is contained in every closed
49:    $F \subseteq X$ with $A \subseteq F$; so it is the smallest closed superset of
50:    $A$, and $A$ is closed if and only if $A = \overline{A}$.
51: 3. $\overline{A} = A \cup A'$.
52: 
53: Claim 2 is recorded here for reference and is discharged in
54: [[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]], where it is what makes the definition of
55: $\overline{A}$ well posed; claims 1 and 3 are proved below. Claim 1 is the form in
56: which the closure is used everywhere afterwards, and clause (d) is what makes a
57: closure computable from a basis rather than from all open sets.
58: 
FILE items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md
41: ## Statement
42: 
43: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a Hausdorff topological space
44: ([[def-hausdorff-space]], [[def-topological-space]]), with compact subsets as in
45: [[def-compact-space]]. Then:
46: 
47: 1. **A point and a disjoint compact set are separated.** If $K \subseteq X$ is
48:    compact and $x \in X \setminus K$, there are $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$ with
49:    $$x \in U, \qquad K \subseteq V, \qquad U \cap V = \varnothing .$$
50: 2. **Two disjoint compact sets are separated.** If $K, L \subseteq X$ are compact
51:    and $K \cap L = \varnothing$, there are $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$ with
52:    $$L \subseteq U, \qquad K \subseteq V, \qquad U \cap V = \varnothing .$$
53: 3. **Compact implies closed.** Every compact subset of $X$ is closed in $X$.
54: 4. **In a compact Hausdorff space the two classes coincide.** If in addition
55:    $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is compact, then a subset of $X$ is compact if and only if
56:    it is closed.
57: 
58: **The proof is written choice-free, and that is not a stylistic preference.** The
59: textbook argument says "for each $y \in K$ choose disjoint open $U_y, V_y$",
60: which is a selection over an arbitrary index set and therefore an appeal to the
61: full Axiom of Choice. What is done below instead is to take the family of **all**
62: open $V$ that admit some open $U \ni x$ disjoint from them — a family cut out by
63: a formula, with nothing selected — extract a finite subcover from it, and only
64: then make finitely many selections, which [[lem-finite-choice]] supplies as a
65: theorem of ZF.
66: 
FILE items/thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness.md
41: ## Statement
42: 
43: Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let $\mathcal{T}_d$ be
44: its metric topology ([[def-metric-topology]]), so that $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$ is a
45: topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and is metrizable
46: ([[def-metrizable-space]]). Then:
47: 
48: 1. $(X,d)$ is a compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]]) **if and only
49:    if** $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$ is a compact topological space
50:    ([[def-compact-space]]).
51: 2. For every $A \subseteq X$: $A$ is a compact subset of the metric space
52:    $(X,d)$ if and only if $A$ is a compact subset of the topological space
53:    $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$, the two readings of "compact subset" being the metric
54:    subspace $(A, d_A)$ ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]) and the topological
55:    subspace $(A, (\mathcal{T}_d)_A)$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
56: 
57: **Nothing here is a coincidence and nothing is transported.** The open-cover
58: condition of [[def-metric-compactness]] quantifies over families of subsets open
59: in $(X,d)$, and by [[def-metric-topology]] those are exactly the members of
60: $\mathcal{T}_d$; so the two conditions are not merely equivalent, they are the
61: same condition written twice. No choice principle is used.
62: 
FILE items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md
41: ## Statement
42: 
43: Let $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ and $(Y, \mathcal{T}_Y)$ be topological spaces
44: ([[def-topological-space]]), and let $\mathbb{R}$ carry its usual topology, the
45: metric topology of $d_{\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$
46: ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-metric-topology]],
47: [[def-metrizable-space]]). Then:
48: 
49: 1. **Continuous images.** If $f : X \to Y$ is continuous
50:    ([[def-continuous-map-top]]) and $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ is compact
51:    ([[def-compact-space]]), then $f[X]$ is a compact subset of $Y$. More
52:    generally, if $K \subseteq X$ is a compact subset of $X$ then $f[K]$ is a
53:    compact subset of $Y$.
54: 2. **Extreme values.** If $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ is compact and nonempty and
55:    $g : X \to \mathbb{R}$ is continuous, then $g[X]$ has a maximum and a minimum
56:    ([[def-max-min]]): there are $x_{\max}, x_{\min} \in X$ with
57:    $$g(x_{\min}) \;\le\; g(x) \;\le\; g(x_{\max}) \qquad \text{for every } x \in X .$$
58: 3. **Compact to Hausdorff.** If $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ is compact, $(Y,
59:    \mathcal{T}_Y)$ is Hausdorff ([[def-hausdorff-space]]) and $f : X \to Y$ is a
60:    continuous bijection, then $f$ is a homeomorphism
61:    ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]).
62: 
63: Nonemptiness in claim 2 is a hypothesis and not an oversight: for
64: $X = \varnothing$ the image is empty and has neither a maximum nor a minimum. **No
65: choice principle is used**: the one selection made below is over a finite index
66: set, where [[lem-finite-choice]] is a theorem of ZF.
67: 
FILE items/thm-continuity-characterisations-top.md
33: ## Statement
34: 
35: Let $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ and $(Y, \mathcal{T}_Y)$ be topological spaces, let
36: $f : X \to Y$ be a function, and let $\mathcal{S}$ be a subbasis for
37: $\mathcal{T}_Y$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]). The following five conditions
38: are equivalent.
39: 
40: - **(a)** $f$ is continuous at every point of $X$ ([[def-continuous-map-top]]).
41: - **(b)** $f^{-1}[V]$ is open in $X$ for every open $V \subseteq Y$.
42: - **(c)** $f^{-1}[F]$ is closed in $X$ for every closed $F \subseteq Y$.
43: - **(d)** $f^{-1}[S]$ is open in $X$ for every $S \in \mathcal{S}$.
44: - **(e)** $f[\overline{A}] \subseteq \overline{f[A]}$ for every $A \subseteq X$,
45:   closures being taken in $X$ and in $Y$ respectively
46:   ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]).
47: 
48: Condition (d) is what makes continuity checkable against a generating family
49: rather than against every open set, and it holds for a *basis* as well, a basis
50: being in particular a subbasis for the topology it generates.
51: 
FILE items/thm-heine-borel-r.md
34: ## Statement
35: 
36: Let $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a \le b$. Then the closed bounded interval
37: $[a,b]$ ([[def-interval]]) is compact ([[def-open-cover-r]]): every family of
38: open subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ whose union contains $[a,b]$ has a finite subfamily
39: whose union already contains $[a,b]$.
40: 
41: The proof is by repeated bisection. Supposing some open cover admits no finite
42: subcover, one halves the interval, keeps a half that still admits none, and
43: iterates; the halves shrink to a point, which the cover does reach, and a single
44: member of the cover then swallows a whole late-stage half. **The halving rule is
45: canonical**, taking the left half whenever the left half works, so the recursion
46: uses [[thm-recursion]] and no choice principle.
47: 
FILE items/thm-heine-borel-rn.md
35: ## Statement
36: 
37: Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $n \ge 1$, let $\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions
38: $n \to \mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it
39: ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:
40: 
41: 1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box
42:    $Q = \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^n : a_k \le x_k \le b_k \text{ for every } k < n \,\}$
43:    is a compact subset of $(\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).
44: 2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of
45:    $(\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\mathbb{R}^n$
46:    ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded
47:    ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).
48: 3. **The real line.** A subset $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of
49:    $(\mathbb{R}, d_{\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$
50:    ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in
51:    $\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.
52: 
53: **No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a
54: time and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely
55: covered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a
56: property of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is
57: available in ZF, while the general "complete and totally bounded implies compact"
58: ([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.
59: 
60: The hypothesis $n \ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines
61: $\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what
62: happens at $n = 0$.
63: 
FILE items/thm-of-archimedean.md
34: ## Statement
35: 
36: Every complete ordered field $F$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) is Archimedean:
37: for every $x \in F$ there is a natural number $n \ge 1$ with $x < n \cdot 1_F$,
38: where $n \cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of the ordered field $F$
39: ([[def-ordered-field]]). Equivalently, the canonical naturals are cofinal in $F$.
40: 
FILE items/thm-one-point-compactification-properties.md
40: ## Statement
41: 
42: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and let
43: $(X^{*}, \mathcal{T}^{*})$ be its one-point compactification, with added point
44: $\infty$ ([[def-one-point-compactification]]). Then:
45: 
46: 1. **$X^{*}$ is compact** ([[def-compact-space]]).
47: 2. **$X$ is an open subspace of $X^{*}$**: $X \in \mathcal{T}^{*}$, and the
48:    subspace topology that $X$ inherits from $X^{*}$
49:    ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]) is $\mathcal{T}$ itself.
50: 3. **$X$ is dense in $X^{*}$** ([[def-dense-top]]) **if and only if $X$ is not
51:    compact.**
52: 4. **$X^{*}$ is Hausdorff** ([[def-hausdorff-space]]) **if and only if $X$ is
53:    locally compact** ([[def-locally-compact-space]]) **and Hausdorff.**
54: 
55: In particular, a locally compact Hausdorff space is an open subspace of a compact
56: Hausdorff space, which is the reason the construction is made. **No choice
57: principle is used**: the only cover thinned below is thinned by the indexed form
58: of [[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]], which returns its own indices.
59: 
FILE items/thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness.md
40: ## Statement
41: 
42: Every ordinal carries the order topology of the membership order on it
43: ([[def-ordinal]], [[def-order-topology-on-a-linearly-ordered-set]]), with the
44: clopen basis $\mathcal{B}_\gamma$ of
45: [[lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal]]. Then:
46: 
47: 1. **Successors are compact.** For every ordinal $\delta$ the successor ordinal
48:    $\delta^{+}$ is compact ([[def-compact-space]]).
49: 2. **Limits are not.** No limit ordinal ([[def-limit-ordinal]]) is compact.
50: 3. **Assuming the Axiom of Countable Choice** ([[def-countable-choice]]): the
51:    first uncountable ordinal $\omega_1$ ([[def-first-uncountable-ordinal]]) is
52:    sequentially compact and countably compact ([[def-compactness-variants]]),
53:    and it is not compact; while $\omega_1 + 1$ is compact
54:    ([[def-ordinal-addition]]).
55: 
56: Claims 1 and 2 are theorems of ZF. Claim 3 spends countable choice twice, both
57: times through cited results that carry the hypothesis in their own statements:
58: [[thm-countable-subsets-of-omega-one-are-bounded]], which supplies the
59: boundedness of at most countable subsets of $\omega_1$, and claim 2 of
60: [[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]], which converts sequential compactness
61: into countable compactness; the extraction of a subsequence below selects
62: nothing, taking least elements throughout.
63: 
FILE items/thm-product-universal-property.md
38: ## Statement
39: 
40: Let $(X_i, \mathcal{T}_i)_{i \in I}$ be topological spaces and let
41: $P := \prod_{i \in I} X_i$ carry the product topology, with projections $\pi_j$
42: ([[def-product-topology]]). Then:
43: 
44: 1. **The projections are continuous**, and the product topology is the coarsest
45:    topology on $P$ making all of them continuous.
46: 2. **Characteristic property.** For every space $Z$ and every function
47:    $h : Z \to P$,
48:    $$h \text{ is continuous } \iff \pi_i \circ h \text{ is continuous for every } i \in I .$$
49:    The functions $\pi_i \circ h$ are the **components** of $h$, and every family
50:    of functions $h_i : Z \to X_i$ arises from exactly one $h$, namely
51:    $h(z)(i) := h_i(z)$.
52: 3. **The projections are open maps** ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]), for
53:    the product topology and for the box topology alike. They need not be closed;
54:    that failure is recorded on this page as a false statement.
55: 4. **Surjectivity.** If every $X_i$ is nonempty then every $\pi_j$ is surjective.
56:    For $I$ a natural number this is a theorem of ZF ([[lem-finite-choice]]); for
57:    an arbitrary $I$ it is the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]), and this
58:    is the only place in the item where a choice principle is used.
59: 
FILE items/thm-quotient-universal-property.md
37: ## Statement
38: 
39: Let $q : X \to Y$ be a quotient map ([[def-quotient-topology]]). Then:
40: 
41: 1. **Characteristic property.** For every space $W$ and every function
42:    $k : Y \to W$,
43:    $$k \text{ is continuous } \iff k \circ q \text{ is continuous} .$$
44: 2. **Factorisation.** Let $f : X \to W$ be continuous and **constant on the
45:    fibres of $q$**, that is $q(x) = q(x')$ implies $f(x) = f(x')$. Then there is
46:    exactly one function $\bar f : Y \to W$ with $\bar f \circ q = f$, and it is
47:    continuous.
48: 3. **Composites.** If $q : X \to Y$ and $p : Y \to Z$ are quotient maps then
49:    $p \circ q : X \to Z$ is a quotient map.
50: 
FILE items/thm-r-uncountable.md
44: ## Statement
45: 
46: Let $\mathbb{R}$ be a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
47: Then $\mathbb{R}$ is **uncountable** ([[def-countable]]): there is no surjection
48: $\mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$, so $\mathbb{R}$ is neither finite nor countably
49: infinite.
50: 
51: The proof is Cantor's original argument of 1874, not the decimal diagonal.
52: Assuming a surjection $f : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$, one builds nested closed
53: intervals $[a_n, b_n]$ with $a_n < b_n$ and $f(n) \notin [a_{n+1}, b_{n+1}]$, and
54: then $\sup\{a_n\}$ is a real number that $f$ misses. **The decimal diagonal is
55: deliberately avoided**: decimal expansions are infinite series, which this
56: library has not yet constructed, so a diagonal proof here would rest on machinery
57: that does not exist. The diagonal argument survives in its non-circular form, on
58: power sets, as Cantor's theorem earlier on this page; see the remarks below.
59: 
60: **The construction uses no choice, and that is what the thirds are for.** Given
61: $[a_n, b_n]$ of length $d$, its three closed thirds
62: $[a_n, a_n + d/3]$, $[a_n + d/3, a_n + 2d/3]$, $[a_n + 2d/3, b_n]$ cannot all
63: contain $f(n)$, because the first and the third are disjoint; the rule takes the
64: **first one in that fixed order** which does not contain $f(n)$. That is a
65: definition by cases, so the whole construction is a single application of the
66: recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]) to one explicitly given function. A version
67: of the argument that says "pick a third avoiding $f(n)$" would be using dependent
68: choice, silently and unnecessarily.
69: 
FILE items/thm-rationals-countable.md
44: ## Statement
45: 
46: $\mathbb{Q} \approx \mathbb{N}$ ([[def-equinumerous]]): the rationals are
47: countably infinite ([[def-countable]]).
48: 
49: **No choice principle is used.** The one place where a reader expects a choice,
50: "pick a representative $a/b$ of each rational", is exactly where
51: [[lem-rat-positive-denominator]] applies: every rational *has* a representative
52: with positive denominator, so the map $(a,b) \mapsto [(a,b)]$ defined on
53: $\mathbb{Z} \times \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ is already **surjective** onto $\mathbb{Q}$,
54: and countability follows from a surjection without ever selecting a
55: representative. The same device handles $\mathbb{Z}$, which is a surjective image
56: of $\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}$ by construction ([[def-integers]]).
57: 
FILE items/thm-tychonoff.md
43: ## Statement
44: 
45: **Assume the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
46: 
47: Let $I$ be a set and let $(X_i, \mathcal{T}_i)_{i \in I}$ be a family of compact
48: topological spaces ([[def-compact-space]], [[def-topological-space]]). Then the
49: product
50: 
51: $$P \;:=\; \prod_{i \in I} X_i$$
52: 
53: with the product topology ([[def-product-topology]]) is compact.
54: 
55: **The Axiom of Choice is spent twice, and both uses are flagged below.** Once
56: inside [[thm-alexander-subbase-lemma]], through Zorn's lemma ([[thm-zorn]]), and
57: once directly at step 2.1, to produce a point of a product of nonempty sets.
58: 

