FILE items/ex-sorgenfrey-line.md
40: ## Example
41: 
42: Let $\mathcal{B} := \{\, [a,b) : a, b \in \mathbb{R},\ a < b \,\}$ be the family
43: of bounded half-open intervals of $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-interval]]). Then:
44: 
45: 1. **$\mathcal{B}$ is a basis** ([[thm-basis-criterion]]) for a topology
46:    $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{S}}$ on $\mathbb{R}$. The space
47:    $(\mathbb{R}, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{S}})$ is the **Sorgenfrey line**, also
48:    called the lower limit topology.
49: 2. **$\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{S}}$ is strictly finer than the usual topology**
50:    ([[def-metrizable-space]], [[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]): every set
51:    open in the usual topology is in $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{S}}$, and $[0,1)$ is in
52:    $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{S}}$ and is not open in the usual topology.
53: 3. **The Sorgenfrey line is first countable** ([[def-first-countable-top]]): for
54:    $x \in \mathbb{R}$ the family
55:    $\{\, [x,\ x + 1/(k+1)) : k \in \mathbb{N} \,\}$ is an at most countable
56:    neighbourhood base at $x$.
57: 4. **It has an at most countable dense subset**, namely the rationals: $\mathbb{Q}$
58:    is dense in $(\mathbb{R}, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{S}})$ ([[def-dense-top]]) and is
59:    at most countable ([[thm-rationals-countable]], [[def-countable]]).
60: 5. **Sequences converge only from the right.** For a sequence $(x_k)$ in
61:    $\mathbb{R}$ and $x \in \mathbb{R}$, $x_k \to x$ in $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{S}}$
62:    ([[def-sequence-convergence-top]]) if and only if for every real
63:    $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$ with
64:    $x \le x_k < x + \varepsilon$ for all $k \ge K$. In particular the sequence
65:    $y_k := x - 1/(k+1)$ converges to $x$ in the usual topology and does not
66:    converge to $x$ in $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{S}}$.
67: 
68: At this point in the reading order, separability has not yet been defined; the
69: later definition [[def-separable-space]] abbreviates claim 4.
70: 
FILE items/fs-local-compactness-is-hereditary.md
40: ## Statement
41: 
42: **False claim:** local compactness ([[def-locally-compact-space]]) is a
43: hereditary property ([[def-hereditary-property]]): every subspace
44: ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]) of a locally compact space is locally compact.
45: 
46: **Where the claim comes from, and what is actually true.** Metrizability is
47: hereditary, and so are several other properties of the same shape, so the
48: expectation is natural. What is true for local compactness is heredity along
49: **open** subspaces and along **closed** subspaces of a locally compact Hausdorff
50: space, and no more. The witness below is the rationals inside the real line,
51: which is neither open nor closed in it.
52: 
FILE items/thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy.md
32: ## Statement
33: 
34: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), with
35: compactness as in [[def-compact-space]] and the variants as in
36: [[def-compactness-variants]]. Then:
37: 
38: 1. **Theorems of ZF.**
39:    - (a) If $X$ is compact it is countably compact and Lindel&ouml;f.
40:    - (b) If $X$ is countably compact and Lindel&ouml;f it is compact.
41:    - (c) If $X$ is compact it is limit point compact.
42:    - (d) If $X$ is countably compact then every countably infinite subset of $X$
43:      ([[def-countable]]) has a limit point in $X$.
44: 2. **Assuming the Axiom of Countable Choice** ([[def-countable-choice]]): if $X$
45:    is sequentially compact it is countably compact.
46: 3. **Assuming the Axiom of Dependent Choice** ([[def-dependent-choice]]): if $X$
47:    is countably compact it is limit point compact.
48: 4. **Assuming the Axiom of Countable Choice, and that every singleton
49:    $\{x\} \subseteq X$ is closed:** if $X$ is limit point compact it is countably
50:    compact.
51: 
52: **Every hypothesis is stated where it is spent.** Claim 1 uses no choice
53: principle at all. Claim 2 spends countable choice once, to pick a point outside
54: each of countably many nonempty sets; claim 4 spends it in the same place; claim 3
55: spends dependent choice once, to extract a countably infinite subset from an
56: infinite set. Each is an upper bound on the cost of the proof given here, never a
57: claim of necessity.
58: 
59: **The hypothesis of claim 4 is written out rather than named.** "Every singleton
60: is closed" is a separation axiom, and separation axioms are not available at this
61: point in the reading order; the condition is used exactly as stated and nothing
62: about the axiom it belongs to is asserted.
63: 
FILE items/thm-finite-products-of-compact-spaces.md
42: ## Statement
43: 
44: For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]) and every family
45: $(X_k)_{k < n}$ of compact topological spaces ([[def-compact-space]],
46: [[def-topological-space]]), the product
47: 
48: $$\prod_{k < n} X_k$$
49: 
50: with the product topology ([[def-product-topology]]) is compact. In particular a
51: binary product $X \times Y$ of compact spaces is compact, and the empty product,
52: a one-point space, is compact.
53: 
54: **No choice principle is used beyond [[lem-finite-choice]]**, which is a theorem
55: of ZF. That is what separates the finite case from the arbitrary one, where the
56: Axiom of Choice is genuinely spent.
57: 
FILE items/thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics.md
42: ## Statement
43: 
44: Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]). Then:
45: 
46: 1. **A neighbourhood base of compact sets.** If $X$ is locally compact
47:    ([[def-locally-compact-space]]) and Hausdorff ([[def-hausdorff-space]]), then
48:    every neighbourhood $N$ of a point $x \in X$ ([[def-neighbourhood-top]])
49:    contains a compact neighbourhood of $x$; so the compact neighbourhoods of $x$
50:    form a neighbourhood base at $x$.
51: 2. **Heredity along open and closed subspaces.** If $X$ is locally compact and
52:    Hausdorff and $S \subseteq X$ is open, then the subspace $S$ is locally
53:    compact ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]). If $X$ is locally compact and
54:    $F \subseteq X$ is closed, then the subspace $F$ is locally compact; no
55:    Hausdorff hypothesis is used for this half.
