    43	## Definition
    44	
    45	**The quotient topology.** Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space
    46	([[def-topological-space]]), let $Y$ be a set and let $q : X \to Y$ be a
    47	surjection ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]). The **quotient topology** on
    48	$Y$ induced by $q$ is the final topology of the one-element family $(q)$
    49	([[def-initial-and-final-topology]]):
    50	
    51	$$\mathcal{T}_q \;:=\; \{\, V \subseteq Y : q^{-1}[V] \in \mathcal{T} \,\} .$$
    52	
    53	That this is a topology is discharged in [[def-initial-and-final-topology]],
    54	where every final topology is verified to satisfy (T1), (T2) and (T3). Dually,
    55	$C \subseteq Y$ is closed in $\mathcal{T}_q$ exactly when $q^{-1}[C]$ is closed
    56	in $X$, because $q^{-1}[Y \setminus V] = X \setminus q^{-1}[V]$.
    57	
    58	**Quotient map.** A surjection $q : X \to Y$ between topological *spaces* is a
    59	**quotient map** (also *identification map*) when the topology of $Y$ is the
    60	quotient topology of $q$, that is when
    61	
    62	$$V \text{ is open in } Y \iff q^{-1}[V] \text{ is open in } X \qquad (V \subseteq Y).$$
    63	
    64	The implication from left to right is exactly continuity of $q$
    65	([[def-continuous-map-top]], [[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]] clause
    66	(b)), so a quotient map is a continuous surjection with the extra property that
    67	its topology is as *fine* as continuity permits. Equivalently, and this form is
    68	used as often: $C$ is closed in $Y$ if and only if $q^{-1}[C]$ is closed in $X$.
    69	
    70	**Saturated sets.** Let $q : X \to Y$ be a surjection. A subset $A \subseteq X$ is
    71	**saturated** with respect to $q$ when
    72	
    73	$$A \;=\; q^{-1}\big[\,q[A]\,\big] ,$$
    74	
    75	equivalently when $A$ is a union of fibres $q^{-1}[\{y\}]$, equivalently when
    76	$x \in A$ and $q(x') = q(x)$ imply $x' \in A$. The set $q^{-1}[q[A]]$ is the
    77	**saturation** of $A$, and it is the smallest saturated set containing $A$. The
    78	map $V \mapsto q^{-1}[V]$ is a bijection from $\mathcal{P}(Y)$ onto the saturated
    79	subsets of $X$, with inverse $A \mapsto q[A]$, since $q$ is surjective; under it
    80	the open sets of the quotient topology correspond exactly to the **saturated open
    81	subsets of $X$**. That correspondence is the working description of the quotient
    82	topology: to know the open sets of $Y$ is to know which open subsets of $X$ are
    83	saturated.
    84	
    85	**Quotient by an equivalence relation.** Let $\sim$ be an **equivalence relation**
    86	on $X$, that is a relation that is reflexive ($x \sim x$), symmetric
    87	($x \sim x'$ implies $x' \sim x$) and transitive ($x \sim x'$ and $x' \sim x''$
    88	imply $x \sim x''$). Write $[x] := \{\, x' \in X : x' \sim x \,\}$ for the
    89	**equivalence class** of $x$; distinct classes are disjoint and their union is
    90	$X$. The **quotient set** is $X/\!\sim\ := \{\, [x] : x \in X \,\}$ and the
    91	**canonical projection** is
    92	
    93	$$\pi : X \to X/\!\sim, \qquad \pi(x) := [x] ,$$
    94	
    95	which is a surjection by construction. The set $X/\!\sim$ always carries the
    96	quotient topology of $\pi$, and $(X/\!\sim,\ \mathcal{T}_\pi)$ is called an
    97	**identification space**. A subset of $X$ is saturated for $\pi$ exactly when it
    98	is a union of equivalence classes.
    99	
   100	**A convenient special case: collapsing a subset.** For $\varnothing \ne B \subseteq X$
   101	let $\sim_B$ be the relation whose classes are $B$ itself and the singletons
   102	$\{x\}$ for $x \notin B$; this is an equivalence relation, its classes being a
   103	partition of $X$. The resulting quotient is written $X/B$, and its canonical
   104	projection is a surjection identifying all of $B$ to a single point and doing
   105	nothing else. Saturated sets for $\sim_B$ are the sets $A$ with $A \cap B \in
   106	\{\varnothing, B\}$.
   107	
   108	**Two conventions used throughout.** First, "quotient map" is a property of a map
   109	*together with the two topologies*, never of the map alone. Second, a quotient
   110	topology is determined by $q$ together with the topology on its domain, and not by
   111	the underlying pair of sets $(X,Y)$: two different surjections onto the same set
   112	can give different topologies, the same surjection gives different topologies when
   113	its domain is retopologised, and where more than one is in play the map is named.
   114	
   115	## Remarks
    26	## Definition
    27	
    28	A real $x$ is **positive** when it has a representative $(a_n)$ together
    29	with a rational $\delta > 0$ and an index $N$ such that $a_n > \delta$
    30	for all $n \ge N$. Define
    31	
    32	$$x \le y \iff x = y \;\text{ or }\; y - x \text{ is positive},$$
    33	
    34	and $|x| = x$ if $x \ge 0$, $|x| = -x$ otherwise.
