items/thm-nonnegative-continuous-with-zero-integral-vanishes.md:68:1.1 Suppose, for contradiction, that $f$ does not vanish identically; since $f \ge 0$, this gives $c \in [a,b]$ with $f(c) > 0$, which is [A1]. [assume-contra, given]
items/thm-nonnegative-continuous-with-zero-integral-vanishes.md:86:- **The nonnegativity on the two outer pieces is cited, not assumed away.** The usual one-line version writes "so $\int_a^b f \ge \int_p^q f$" without saying why; what makes that step legitimate is that $f \ge 0$ on $[a,p]$ and on $[q,b]$ too, so both of those integrals are $\ge 0$ ([[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]]). Without a sign hypothesis outside $[p,q]$ the argument would fail.
