items/thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function.md:51:## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function.md:57:[L2] For a partition $P = (n,t)$ of $[a,b]$ and bounded $u$: $U(u,P) - L(u,P) = \sum_{i<n}\bigl(M_i(u) - m_i(u)\bigr)\Delta_i$ with $\Delta_i > 0$ and $\sum_{i<n}\Delta_i = b - a$, and $M_i(u) - m_i(u) = \omega_u(I_i) = \sup\{\,|u(x)-u(y)| : x,y \in I_i\,\}$ ([[def-darboux-sums]], [[def-oscillation]], [[def-partition-and-refinement]], [[def-finite-sum]], [[lem-finite-sum-laws]]).
items/thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function.md:65:[L6] Finite sums: additivity, scaling and monotonicity in the terms ([[def-finite-sum]], [[lem-finite-sum-laws]], clauses 1, 2 and 4).
items/thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function.md:87:4.1 In both cases $\bigl(M_i(h)-m_i(h)\bigr)\Delta_i \le \varepsilon\,\Delta_i + \bigl(2K/\delta\bigr)\Omega_i\Delta_i$: in the first case the second summand is nonnegative and the first alone dominates, and in the second case $(2K/\delta)\Omega_i\Delta_i \ge 2K\Delta_i$ dominates by itself. [step 3.1, step 3.2, L7]
items/thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function.md:89:5.1 Summing over $i < n$ with [L6] and using $\sum_{i<n}\Delta_i = b-a$ and [L2] gives $U(h,P)-L(h,P) \le \varepsilon(b-a) + (2K/\delta)\bigl(U(f,P)-L(f,P)\bigr)$. [step 4.1, L2, L6, L7]
items/thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function.md:91:6.1 By step 2.2 the second summand is below $(2K/\delta)\delta^{2} = 2K\delta$, and $\delta < \varepsilon$ by step 1.2, so $U(h,P)-L(h,P) < \varepsilon\,(b-a+2K)$. [step 2.2, step 5.1, L7]
items/thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function.md:99:- **Step 4.1 is what replaces the usual split of the index range.** The classical proof separates the indices into a good set $G$ and a bad set $B$ and sums over each; the finite-sum toolkit used here is that of [[lem-finite-sum-laws]], stated for $\sum_{i<n}$ and carrying no clause that splits a range into a subset and its complement, so the split is carried instead by a single inequality valid at *every* index, whose two summands are exactly the two contributions. The bound obtained is the same one.
items/thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function.md:101:- **The hypothesis $f[\,[a,b]\,] \subseteq [m,M]$ is what makes $\varphi\circ f$ defined at all**, and $m,M$ exist because an integrable $f$ is bounded ([[def-darboux-sums]]). Taking $[m,M]$ to be any interval containing the range of $f$ is legitimate and changes nothing, since a continuous function on a larger compact interval restricts to a continuous one.

