    31	---
    32	
    33	## Definition
    34	
    35	Let $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, with open sets as in
    36	[[def-open-and-closed-in-r]].
    37	
    38	- An **open cover** of $K$ is a family $\mathcal{U}$ of open subsets of
    39	  $\mathbb{R}$ with $K \subseteq \bigcup \mathcal{U}$.
    40	- A **subcover** of $\mathcal{U}$ is a subfamily $\mathcal{V} \subseteq
    41	  \mathcal{U}$ that is still an open cover of $K$.
    42	- A subfamily $\mathcal{V} \subseteq \mathcal{U}$ is **finite** when
    43	  $\mathcal{V} = \varnothing$ or there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and members
    44	  $U_0, \dots, U_n$ of $\mathcal{U}$ with $\mathcal{V} = \{U_0, \dots, U_n\}$;
    45	  repetitions in the list are allowed and harmless.
    46	- $K$ is **compact** when every open cover of $K$ has a finite subcover:
    47	  for every open cover $\mathcal{U}$ of $K$, either $K = \varnothing$ and the
    48	  empty subfamily covers it, or there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
    49	  $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with
    50	  $$K \subseteq U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n .$$
    51	- $K$ is **sequentially compact** when every sequence $(x_k)$ of reals with
    52	  $x_k \in K$ for all $k \in \mathbb{N}$ ([[def-sequence]]) has a subsequence
    53	  converging ([[def-real-limit]]) to some point of $K$; equivalently, when every
    54	  such sequence has a subsequential limit ([[def-subsequential-limit]]) that
    55	  lies in $K$.
    56	
    57	**Compactness is a property of $K$ alone.** The covering families range over open
    58	subsets of $\mathbb{R}$, not over sets open in some other ambient space, so the
    59	notion defined here is compactness of $K$ as a subset of $\mathbb{R}$. Nothing
    60	below relativises it to a smaller ambient field; where an ordered field other
    61	than $\mathbb{R}$ is meant, as in
    62	[[fs-closed-bounded-compact-without-completeness]], the whole vocabulary is set
    63	up again there for that field.
    64	
    65	**$\varnothing$ is compact and sequentially compact.** The empty subfamily covers
    66	it, and there is no sequence with all terms in $\varnothing$, so both conditions
    67	hold vacuously.
    68	
    69	## Remarks
    70	
    71	- **Why "finite" is spelled out by listing.** A finite subfamily is described
    72	  here as one that can be written $\{U_0, \dots, U_n\}$ with $n \in \mathbb{N}$,
    73	  which is exactly the form every proof on this page produces or consumes: the
    74	  bisection argument of [[thm-heine-borel-r]] produces a one-member list, and
    75	  the arguments of [[lem-compact-implies-closed-and-bounded-r]] consume a list by
    76	  taking a maximum over it ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]]). Since $\mathbb{N}$
    77	  contains $0$, the shortest nonempty list is $\{U_0\}$.
    78	
    79	- **The two notions are not defined to be equivalent, and their equivalence is a
    80	  theorem.** For subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ it is
    81	  [[thm-compact-iff-sequentially-compact-r]]; both of its implications run
    82	  through the characterisation of compactness by closed and bounded, and its
    83	  forward implication additionally uses Bolzano-Weierstrass. Neither implication
    84	  is formal.
    85	
    86	- **Compactness is not inherited by subsets, but by closed subsets.** A closed
    87	  subset of a compact set is compact, which is immediate from
    88	  [[thm-heine-borel-characterisation-r]] once that is available, whereas
    89	  $(0,1) \subseteq [0,1]$ shows that an arbitrary subset of a compact set need
    90	  not be compact.
    91	
    92	- **The empty cover.** If $K \ne \varnothing$ then no open cover of $K$ is
    93	  empty, so the case distinction in the definition of compactness only ever
    94	  matters for $K = \varnothing$; it is written out so that the definition does
    95	  not quietly assume $K$ nonempty.
