    39	
    40	## Statement
    41	
    42	**Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice** ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let
    43	$(A_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ be a family of at most countable sets
    44	([[def-countable]]) indexed by $\mathbb{N}$. Then
    45	
    46	$$U = \bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} A_n$$
    47	
    48	is at most countable.
    49	
    50	**The hypothesis $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is not decoration and it is not removable.**
    51	It is spent at exactly one step, step 3.1 below, where one surjection
    52	$\mathbb{N} \to A_n$ is selected for every $n$ at once. Each $A_n$ has such
    53	surjections, in general many of them, and the countability assumption provides no
    54	rule for singling one out. Without some choice principle the theorem is not
    55	available at all: ZF alone does not prove it, **conditionally on the consistency
    56	of ZF**, as recorded among this page's false statements and discussed in the
    57	remarks below, where that item is named and linked. The
    58	consistency hypothesis is not a formality and cannot be dropped: the separation
    59	rests on an external independence result that this library quotes rather than
    60	proves, and it cannot be stated without it.
    61	
    62	## Facts & Assumptions
    63	
    64	**Given:** A family $(A_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of at most countable sets, its union $U = \bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} A_n$, and the Axiom of Countable Choice as an explicit hypothesis.
    65	
    66	[L1] Finite, countably infinite, at most countable; $\varnothing$ is finite ([[def-countable]]).
    67	
    68	[L2] A nonempty set $X$ is at most countable if and only if there is a surjection $\mathbb{N} \to X$ ([[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]]).
    69	
    70	[L3] $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$: for every family $(X_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets there is $f$ with $f(n) \in X_n$ for all $n$ ([[def-countable-choice]]).
    71	
    72	[L4] There is a bijection $\beta : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}$ ([[thm-n-cross-n-countable]], [[def-equinumerous]]).
    73	
    74	[L5] Every nonempty subset of $\mathbb{N}$ has a least element ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]).
    75	
    76	[L6] A composition of surjections is a surjection ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
    77	
    78	## Proof
    79	
    80	**Proof technique:** direct.
