    34	
    35	## Statement
    36	
    37	Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), let $A \subseteq X$ and let
    38	$(A,d_A)$ be the metric subspace ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]). Then:
    39	
    40	1. **Relative openness is a trace.** A set $V \subseteq A$ is open in $(A,d_A)$
    41	   ([[def-metric-topology]]) if and only if $V = U \cap A$ for some $U$ open in
    42	   $(X,d)$.
    43	2. **Compactness read in the ambient space.** $A$ is a compact subset of $X$
    44	   ([[def-metric-compactness]]), that is $(A,d_A)$ is a compact metric space, if
    45	   and only if for every family $\mathcal{U}$ of open subsets of $X$ with
    46	   $A \subseteq \bigcup \mathcal{U}$ there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
    47	   $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with $A \subseteq U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n$,
    48	   or else $A = \emptyset$.
    49	3. **The same in indexed form.** $A$ is a compact subset of $X$ if and only if
    50	   for every set $I$ and every family $(U_i)_{i \in I}$ of open subsets of $X$
    51	   with $A \subseteq \bigcup_{i \in I} U_i$ there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
    52	   indices $i_0, \dots, i_n \in I$ with
    53	   $A \subseteq U_{i_0} \cup \dots \cup U_{i_n}$, or else $A = \emptyset$.
    54	
    55	Claim 3 is the form used by almost every later proof on this page, because a
    56	cover is usually produced by a rule that attaches an open set to each point or to
    57	each index, and a set of open sets forgets that rule. No choice principle is used
    58	anywhere below; the one place a selection is made is over a finite index set, and
    59	[[lem-finite-choice]] is a theorem of ZF.
    60	
    61	## Facts & Assumptions
    62	
    63	**Given:** A metric space $(X,d)$, a subset $A \subseteq X$, and the metric subspace $(A, d_A)$ with $d_A$ the restriction of $d$ to $A \times A$.
    64	
    65	[L1] Balls of a subspace are traces of ambient balls: $B_A(a,r) = B_X(a,r) \cap A$ for $a \in A$ and $r > 0$ ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]], [[def-metric-ball]]).
    66	
    67	[L2] A subset $W$ of a metric space is open exactly when every point of $W$ has a ball around it contained in $W$ ([[def-metric-topology]]).
    68	
    69	[L3] Open balls are open, and an arbitrary union of open sets is open ([[thm-metric-open-set-algebra]]).
    70	
    71	[L4] $(A,d_A)$ is compact exactly when every family of sets open in $(A,d_A)$ whose union is $A$ has a finite subfamily whose union is $A$; a family is finite when it is empty or listable as $\{V_0, \dots, V_n\}$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).
    72	
    73	[L5] A function with domain a natural number all of whose values are nonempty sets has a choice function, and this is a theorem of ZF ([[lem-finite-choice]]).
    74	
    75	## Proof
    76	
    77	**Proof technique:** direct.
    78	
    79	1.1 Suppose $U$ is open in $(X,d)$ and put $V := U \cap A$; for $a \in V$ we have $a \in U$, so there is $r > 0$ with $B_X(a,r) \subseteq U$, whence $B_A(a,r) = B_X(a,r) \cap A \subseteq U \cap A = V$, and $V$ is open in $(A,d_A)$. [L1, L2]
    80	
    81	1.2 Conversely let $V \subseteq A$ be open in $(A,d_A)$, and let $\mathcal{W} := \{\, B_X(a,r) : a \in V,\ r > 0,\ B_X(a,r) \cap A \subseteq V \,\}$, a family cut out by a property of the pair $(a,r)$ and not by any selection; put $U := \bigcup \mathcal{W}$. [L1, construct]
    82	
    83	2.1 $U$ is open in $(X,d)$, being a union of open balls. [L3, step 1.2]
    84	
    85	2.2 $U \cap A \subseteq V$, since every member $B_X(a,r)$ of $\mathcal{W}$ satisfies $B_X(a,r) \cap A \subseteq V$ by the defining condition of $\mathcal{W}$. [step 1.2]

